A twenty-eight-year-old chef was found dead in a San Francisco store
A twenty-eight-year-old chef who had recently moved to San Francisco this week was the steps of a local store.
However, the victim’s brother says he doesn’t know who wants to hurt him and adds, “He’s finally starting to thrive.”
The body found in Westfield Mall Wednesday morning was identified as a twenty-eight-year-old resident of San Francisco Frank Galicia, the medical examiner's office said.
The San Francisco Police Department said it was a violent death and an investigation was underway.
The victim's brother, Louis Galicia, told ABC KGO in San Francisco that Frank, who had previously been rank-and-file chef in Boston, got his dreaming job as chef at the Sons & Daughters restaurant six months ago.
Sons & Daughters, a spokesperson for the restaurant, said they were "shocked and devastated" by his death.
"We have a small team that works as a closely connected family. We will miss it very much," the spokesperson said.
At this difficult time, our thoughts and sorrows belong to Frank's family and friends.
Louis Galicia stated that Frank slept in hostels at first, but that recently "he finally began to thrive."
"He's found an apartment, he's been dating a girl," Louis Galicia told KGO.
Louis Galicia said he didn't wonder who wanted to hurt his younger brother.
He had a kind soul and a big heart.
When he wanted to get in touch with his family, he always cooked us food, he made us dinner," Louis Galicia said.
He never wanted to take part in an argument.
He was a brother who took things as they are.
“Even if everything else in the world were bad, he was an ungrinded diamond that illuminated every day,” he said.
If you have any information, please call SFPD at 415-575-4444.
Czech Railways has invested more than £31 billion in vehicle modernisation.
They have air conditioning and new toilets.
In the last six years, the state carrier has invested more than 31 billion crowns in the modernization of vehicles.
A third of the cars are currently equipped with air conditioning, about half of the trains have a new system of closed toilets.
However, amounts for modernization in individual years, with the exception of 2012, are gradually decreasing.
In the last six years, Czech Railways has invested more than 31 billion crowns in the modernization of vehicles.
A third of the cars are currently equipped with air conditioning, about half of the trains have a new system of closed toilets.
The money spent was reflected in the reduction of the average age by two years for locomotives and separate units and by six years for classical wagons.
A larger share of the investment was given by the carrier in regional transport, where about 60 percent of that money was directed, 40 percent then the company invested in the recovery of vehicles for long-distance transport.
The average age of locomotives and units is currently 19 years old.
However, upgraded older vehicles are counted as completely new in this indicator.
If the modernization of vehicles were not foreseen, the average age of ČD propulsion vehicles would increase by ten years.
Slightly older are, on average, personal carriages, whose average age at the loading of modernizations is 23 years.
If only the age from the date of manufacture were taken into account, the average age of these vehicles would be 33.4 years.
However, in recent years, classic kits composed of wagons and locomotives have been gradually disappearinging from the Czech and global railway and are being replaced by motor or electric units.
Between 2011 and 2015, the locomotive decreased from 280 to 223, similarly the number of classic wagons dropped from 2716 in 2011 to 2132 in the middle of this year.
In particular, the carrier disposed of old vehicles equipped with leatherette seats and doors without the possibility of blocking the opening while driving.
From January to July, the carrier scrapped 110 of these cars.
From the investments that ČD plans to make in the near future, it is possible, for example, to mention the purchase of 50 wagons for speeds of up to 200 kilometers per hour, which the carrier plans to deploy on the international line Prague - Bratislava - Budapest, or a framework contract for the purchase of electric units for the domestic express line.
The contract foresees the collection of up to 30 pieces of motor and 20 pieces of electrical units.
Authorities have known about illegal advertising for a quarter of a year, they can't get rid of it.
We're completely helpless, they admit.
For a quarter of a year, the editors of the online daily Aktuálně.cz have been following the story of a billboard in southern Bohemia, which now attracts motorists to visit Lipno, where there is not only an interesting tourist walk in treetops, but also an Olympic park.
Highway administrators know they're standing there black, but they're saying they can't do anything about it.
There are hundreds of similar ads across the country, maybe thousands.
According to officials, it is not possible to remove illegal mobile billboards immediately.
A complex bureaucratic wheel is needed first.
In the meantime, however, the company changes its name and the office is short for it.
Or it is enough to move the ad by a few meters and the official process has to start again.
A quarter of a year ago, the Road and Motorway Directorate admitted: at the main route from České Budějovice to Lipno, an advertising billboard stands on our land by the state road.
However, even three months were not enough for officials to get rid of the unauthorised portable structure with a giant advertising poster.
It's as if no one cares that he's standing next to the road, in a fifty-foot protection zone, where there's none to stand for safety.
Road administrators say they simply can't cope with a billboard because of a leaky law.
Like loading him up and taking him away.
"We can't just afford that," says Nina Ledvinová, a spokeswoman for the Road and Motorway Directorate, referring to the complex bureaucratic wheel, which must be preceded by law.
The case of this particular billboard, whose fate has been closely monitored by the online daily Aktuálně.cz in the spring, is not unique and points to a general problem that the state and the authorities cannot solve effectively and quickly.
In South Bohemia alone, editors counted dozens of similar mobile advertisements placed against the rules just next to or near the road.
There are other countries the same way.
"We have trouble forcing the companies to remove the banners," confirms Jiří Klása, the head of the transport department of the Regional Authority in České Budějovice, whose colleagues deal with similar stories.
According to Klása, a leaky law is to blame, which binds officials.
At first, they have to tell the billboard owner to put the ad away.
But company bosses are often incomparable or they know the trick that Aktuálně.cz described in the past when it became more interested in the issue: it is enough to move the mobile stand with advertising by a few meters, and officials can throw the previous call for the removal of black advertising into the basket.
That's because the office has to determine exactly where an illegal billboard stands.
"If someone shifts it and the coordinates suddenly disagree, it is viewed as a new device and we have to address the owner again," explains Jiří Klása.
The new law won't help.
The change could be brought by a new law, which from September next year will ban billboards on highways and limit them even on 1st-class roads.
Critics point out that the rules for combating illegal and easy-to-transfer ads will remain the same as before.
It won't be so relieved by the authorities.
Thus, companies can still earn tens to hundreds of millions of crowns a year on rentals of illegal areas, and no one can.
For example, the billboard appealing to Lipno belongs to Commerz Billboard.
Officials know her well: the České Budějovice municipality is currently conducting three administrative proceedings with her for black-built billboards.
The company faces a three-hundred-thousand-dollar fine in any proceedings.
These are multiple advertisements that were, without permission, placed in the protection zone of the 1st class road.
Proceedings are not yet concluded, says Jaroslav Mráz, head of the transport department of the České Budějovice municipality.
The ad, which Aktuálně.cz is interested in, does not yet figure among these cases, but will most likely be added to them soon.
According to ŘSD spokeswoman Nina Ledvinová, the company did not respond to repeated written calls for removal, and the case thus reaches the table of regional and then probably municipal officials.
Commerz Billboard is one of those suspicious companies that are unable to contact.
It does not take over the post office and canceled the official headquarters in Žižkov more than six years ago.
It's not even clear who's behind it.
In the past, the company's post office went to the South Bohemian businessman Anton Fischer, who was famous for a sharp dispute with the head of the Czech Hyundai brand representation.
Fischer, whose company Euro Billboard leases and offers areas from Comerz Billboard to advertisers, has rejected any connection in the past.
This spring, when the ŘSD became interested in Lipno advertising at Aktuálně.cz, Fischer had civil servants send a letter for the mysterious company Commerz Billboard to his pension, where the new company is supposedly based.
Even from this address, however, no one has contacted highway administrators to this day.
Futile fight.
Similar silence or opaque changes in settlements are not uncommon.
A few years ago, we gradually fined one company for two million crowns.
But it changed its name and ID and there was nothing to deal with.
The executive returned the case, the commercials stood on, perhaps in other places.
We are quite powerless against such companies and their business practices.
It's like fighting windmills, one of the officials admits on condition of anonymity.
So far, no one has addressed the amendment to the law in this regard.
Currently.cz spoke with several MPs who were interested in the problem.
So far, they haven't exerted any activity.
On the contrary, there were deputies who wanted to extend the deadline for the preservation of billboards on major Czech roads.
The government rejected it.
Billboards are not only a sensitive and somewhat schizophrenic topic not only for nationwide, but also for regional and municipal politicians.
On the one hand, they know about the social demand for their restrictions, on the other hand, they themselves need them for advertising in election campaigns.
I don't give the relatively established impression that when something doesn't work, you have to change the laws.
Farmers will harvest less grain this year, but more rapeseed and poppy seeds, statisticians estimate.
According to estimates by the Czech Statistical Office, cereal harvests will be 6.7 percent lower this year than last year.
Yet this year's crop exceeds the average over the past five and ten years.
On the other hand, the harvest of canola (by 3.9 percent) and poppies (2.5 percent) will be higher.
Cereal harvests will be 6.7 percent lower this year than last year.
Farmers will harvest 7.2 million tonnes of grain.
This is according to an estimate published by the Czech Statistical Office on July 15.
The initial estimate a month ago was more pessimistic and projected a tenth decline compared to last year.
However, last year’s cereal harvest was above average.
This year's crop exceeds the average over the past five or ten years.
On the contrary, this year's rape and poppy harvest will be higher.
Rapeseed growers reap 3.9 percent more than a year ago, when they got 1,256 million tons under the roof.
This year's poppy crop could be 2.5 percent higher than last year's.
The projected decrease in cereal production is due both to a reduction in the hectare yield of 3.7 percent to 5.71 tonnes per hectare and a decrease in the crop area.
Farmers have sowed three percent fewer fields this year.
The winter wheat, which is the most important cereal for the Czech Republic, is estimated to harvest 3.3 percent less this year.
Farmers put her in multiple fields.
However, a lower hectare yield, which drops to 6.04 tonnes, will mark the decline.
Roughly one-fifth decrease is predicted by statisticians in spring barley, which is most often used for the production of beer.
The reduction in crops is due to a 15.2 percent reduction in sowing areas, but also a lower hectare yield.
Winter barley, on the other hand, is produced by 6.3 percent more.
There will also be a harvest of rye.
Rapeseed crop is estimated by statisticians at 1.305 million tonnes, which is more than last year.
Soup areas expanded by 7.3 percent.
However, the hectare yield will be 3.2 percent lower than last year.
This year's rape crop will exceed the 10-year average by 13.3 percent, sown on an area of one-third more extensive than a decade ago.
In the last four years there have also been poppy fields.
This year, farmers put it on 35,543 hectares.
Compared to 2008, when poppies were grown in the Czech Republic on a record 70 000 hectares, however, there are halve of fields.
Thanks to the expansion of seed areas, this year's poppy harvest will be richer.
It will rise 2.5 per cent to 27 423 tons compared to last year.
However, the hectare yield will be 6.1 percent lower.
Olomouc police charge two men for knife and machete attack, court sent them to custody
Police have charged two attackers who attacked a thirty-five-year-old man with a knife and machete in the centre of Olomouc on Tuesday.
"Although the victim tried to escape, they caused him a cut and cutting injury, which required treatment in the hospital and further treatment," police spokeswoman Marie Štrbáková said.
Both obvious offenders were arrested immediately after the attack by police officers.
The plaintiff filed the court's motion to take them into custody, and the court upheld him on Friday.
Both men face between three and ten years in prison.
Police have charged two detainees of assaulting a thirty-five-year-old man in the centre of Olomouc with a knife and machete.
The attackers caused him cuts and cuts, the man ended up in hospital.
The attack was preceded by an incident between two groups of people that happened at noon on Svoboda Avenue on Tuesday, police said.
The perpetrators ended up in custody, Olomouc police spokeswoman Marie Štrbáková said.
A mass skirmish between two families took place in the area of the garden in front of Tratorie bar in the immediate vicinity of the center of Olomouc.
After an aggressive verbal dispute, two attackers attacked a thirty-five-year-old man using a knife and machete.
Although the assaulted man tried to flee, they caused him cut and cut wounds, which required treatment in hospital and other treatments, a police spokeswoman said.
During the scuffle, which attracted a lot of attention in the city center and due to which several police patrols and officers had to intervene on the spot, it was family disputes.
Police have charged a thirty-four-year-old man from Slovakia and a thirty-seven-year-old man from Olomouc with attempted grievous bodily harm and rioting.
