The Clintons earned $10.6 million last year.
Trump refuses to release his tax returns.
Hillary Clinton and her husband, ex-president Bill Clinton, earned €10.6 million last year.
More than a million dollars were donated to Clinton for charity.
Most of Clinton's revenue - over six million dollars - is for their speeches.
Trump refuses to publish his tax returns.
U.S. Democrat candidates for the presidency, Hillary Clinton, released her tax statements on Friday, increasing pressure on her Republican rival, Donald Trump, to do the same.
Hillary Clinton and her husband, ex-president Bill Clinton made £10.6 million last year.
Most of Clinton's revenue - over six million dollars - is for their speeches, mostly from before April 2015, when Clinton launched her campaign before the election.
The tax report showed that the Clintons paid a federal income tax of 34.2 percent last year, as well as income tax at the state and local levels of nine percent.
In total, they paid 43.2 percent of their income in taxes.
But Trump has a problem with a similar move, so far he has not decided to publish the tax return.
There is a certain possibility that Donald Trump pays no taxes at all, Clinton tweeted a few minutes after the publication of her return.
Clinton has repeatedly gone back to secrecy around Trump's income, among other things to raise doubts about whether her opponent is really as wealthy as she claims, the Associated Press reported.
Trump's fortune is estimated at billions of dollars.
Lower earnings would hurt the reputation of the Republican nominee as a successful businessmaker.
Senator Tim Kaine, who in tandem with the former first lady is running for the post of vice president, today released a report on his tax levies over the past decade.
Together with his wife Anne Holton, they gave 7.5 percent of their earnings to charity and paid 25.6 percent of their earnings in taxes last year.
In Nigeria, polio erupted again.
Islamists from Boko Haram have defended consistent vaccinations and killed doctors.
There have been reappearances of polio in Nigeria.
In the northeast of the country, the government declared an extraordinary vaccination, as two children infected with the disease became paralyzed here.
Vaccines were transported by helicopters.
There were Islamist terrorists from Boko Haram in the affected area who defended the vaccination and even killed doctors.
According to the World Health Organization (WHO), the virus was circulating undetected.
In the northeast of Nigeria, an extraordinary vaccination of polio children will begin, as two children infected with the disease have been paralyzed there.
In Africa, polio was last diagnosed in Somalia two years ago.
The Nigerian government has announced that a million children will be inoculated in Borno state, and another four million in surrounding states.
Nigerian military helicopters on Friday quickly delivered polio vaccines to the affected northeastern part of the country, where Islamist Boko Haram terrorists are active.
The World Health Organization (WHO) said the virus had been circulating undetected for many years.
Extremists from Boko Haram have defended vaccinations in the past and even killed doctors.
Their attacks also made it impossible on Thursday for doctors to get into areas where polio children were detected.
Both affected toddlers came with refugees from part of Nigeria newly liberated from Boko Haram.
The director of the WHO section responsible for polio eradication, Michel Zaffran, told the BBC that vaccination in Nigeria will begin as early as next week.
The Islamic Radical Group Boko Haram has long operated in the state of Borno.
The area was only made available to international organizations and government earlier this year, when Boko Haram managed to significantly weaken in this part of the country.
Nigerian Health Minister Isaac Adewole has admitted that the new polish cases are very disappointing for the country because Nigeria wanted to announce next year that polio was eradicated.
The WHO cautioned that it is possible that, although the disease did not appear for two years, it nevertheless spread among children.
Children under the age of five will now be vaccinated.
In 2012, Nigeria accounted for half of all polio cases in the world.
While polio was still in 125 countries in 1988, it now persists in only three areas - Afghanistan and Pakistan and northern Nigeria.
The pope met with former prostitutes in Rome.
He listened to the troubled stories of fallen women.
Pope Francis met with twenty former prostitutes who, thanks to the police, escaped from a gang of pimps.
The pope listened to their difficult life stories and urged them to look to the future with confidence.
Francis wanted to draw attention to human trafficking in this meeting and call for an intensified fight against it.
Pope Francis met with twenty former prostitutes in Rome who were freed by police from the power of a gang of pimps.
For more than an hour, the head of the Catholic Church listened to the troubled stories of fallen women and subsequently urged them to look with confidence into the future, a Vatican spokesman said.
Among the former prostitutes Francis visited were six women from Romania, four from Albania, seven from Nigeria and one from Tunisia, Italy and Ukraine.
Their average age is around 30.
According to the Vatican, they were subjected to cruel physical violence during forced prostitution.
With the visit, Francis wanted to draw attention to human trafficking and call for an intensifying fight against him, the Pope's spokesman said.
Francis often draws attention to the fates of people on the margins of society.
On Thursday, for example, he had lunch with Syrian refugees who arrived at the Vatican this April and June thanks to his personal invitation.
France strikes jihadist factories.
Without the help of Muslims, however, it will not be possible.
France should be assisted by Muslims themselves in the fight against Radical Islamism.
Moreover, the government of Prime Minister Manuel Valls is preparing fundamental changes in the functioning of mosques and the education of clergy.
We've closed 20 mosques and prayer rooms since last December.
And more will follow, the interior minister has announced.
There are over a hundred jihadists in the country.
“If Muslims don’t assist the state in the fight against those who violate public freedom, it will become increasingly difficult for France to guarantee freedom,” Valls said.
He intends, in particular, to preach in mosques and prayer rooms only imams who were educated in France.
At the same time, he wants to stop funding for mosques from abroad.
At the moment, about 20 percent of the money for the construction of new mosques comes from abroad, mostly from Morocco, Algeria and Gulf countries.
Because of this, preachers who can sow radicalism in France come to the mosques.
"Our country needs to show that Islam is compatible with democracy," the prime minister added.
The key that will ensure his achievement of these goals will be the new French Islamic Foundation.
It should act as a bridge between a secular state and a Muslim minority.
No more money from abroad.
The French Foundation for Islam, founded in 2005 by then-Prime Minister Dominique de Villepin.
Already at that time, the foundation was supposed to contribute to the transparency of financial flows between mosques and the countries that finance their construction.
However, internal contradictions and rivalry soon struck the institution.
"It's a total failure," Valls said recently.
On the French political scene, after the attack on Charlie Hebdo magazine in January 2015, there was already a debate about a new institution, which will replace the non-functional foundation.
Other terrorist attacks carried out by radicalized Islamists in the name of the Islamic State have hastened everything.
Bernard Cazeneuve, France’s interior minister, has recently announced that a new foundation will be established in October.
It will have two main tasks - to ensure the financing of mosques from domestic sources and also oversee imams.
For now, it is unclear where he will take the money not only for the construction of new mosques, but also for various educational centers.
However, a number of proposals are being considered.
One of them is a special fee or tax on halal food.
In practice, this would be paid for in practice by sellers of halal food, for the provision of certification.
Some of that money would then go to the foundation's cash register.
A contribution of ten to twenty euros (270 to 540 crowns) could then be paid by French pilgrims going to Mecca.
Every year there are about 30 000 of them on a traditional pilgrimage to Saudi Arabia.
And not just that.
According to the prime minister, in mosques in the future, only preachers who have undergone special educational programs at French universities should operate in mosques.
This should ensure that the sermon is consistent with democracy.
We'll close radical mosques.
At the same time, the French government is campaigning against radicals.
And others will follow, the interior minister recently announced.
There is no place in France for those who proclaim hatred in mosques and do not respect certain principles of our country, such as equality between women and men.
About 2500 mosques and prayers are estimated to be operating in the country.
It is estimated that about 120 of them are under the influence of radical Islamism.
Since 2012, authorities have expelled about 80 imams, and several other dozens are considering it.
However, not everyone thinks that the government’s actions can intervene significantly against radicalism.
Those who want to radicalize will do the same.
This does not happen in mosques, young people are radicalized on the Internet, said Severine Labat, an expert at the National Center for Scientific Research.
Drawner Kelemen lost in Round 1 of the sprint with star Baugé.
To advance to the eight-finals, the Czech track cyclist must fight in repair rides.
Track cyclist Pavel Kelemen at the Olympic Games in Rio de Janeiro, after progressing from sprint qualifiers among the top eighteen in the first round, losed a duel with the French great Grégory Baugém, the defender of silver from the previous games in London.
