Young doctors pushing: They want a fresh breeze in the industry
Representatives of junior doctors met to approve new steps in their dispute over the new treaty.
The Council of Young Physicians (JDC) at the British Medical Association (BMA) must assert full advice on support for other sectoral events in early September.
JDC says ministers have not been able to resolve questions about the treaty.
Young doctors and medical students voted in July to reject a treaty agreed with BMA.
58% of its members who participated in the election were rejected.
In a letter to members released Thursday night, JDC President Ellen McCourt said the government had remained "quiet" about the issues, leading to the rejection of the contract.
She said: "In this light, the JDC's executive body, by voting, decided to reject the proposed new treaty altogether and call for new formal negotiations on all issues."
In response to the government's silence, the JDC Executive Board today formally requested a special meeting of the BMA Board in order to approve a running program of escalated industry events from the beginning of September.
The dispute led young doctors this year to announcement into six strikes, including the first general strikes in NHS history.
BMA's junior doctors' leader, Dr. Johann Malawana, resigned after a decision rejecting the agreed terms of a treaty recommended by BMA.
He told BMA members in pre-vote meetings in front of 54,000 young doctors and medical students that the deal was good and should be accepted.
After the vote, the health minister said the treaty would impose a burden on medics in England.
Russia and Turkey: An alliance of the “incompatible”?
It was a gesture that ended in crisis.
The leaders of Russia and Turkey met on Tuesday to shake hands and announce a formal end to an eight-month-long verbal war and economic sanctions.
But while Vladimir Putin greeted the Turkish counterparty in the sumptuous hall of the palace in St. Petersburg, I had the impression that Ankara wanted more for this reconciliation.
There's been a handshake, yes.
But Putin didn't smile and barely radiated warmth from him, even by his measured standards.
Recep Tayyip Erdogan, on the other hand, has repeatedly talked about his “dear friend” Mr Putin – five times, according to one of the reports.
I didn't count.
He has also vowed that relations with Russia will not only return to its level before the crisis, but they will become even better.
The next day, one of the local newspapers wrote that Erdogan had acted as if nothing evil had ever happened.
In my opinion, his enthusiasm revealed the exact opposite.
But the lingering cold that radiated from Putin showed that the Russian leader had forgotten nothing.
The real cause of the crisis was the first thing he mentioned in his opening speech: the shooting down of a Russian warplane by Turkey on the Syrian border.
Moscow's reaction back then was furious.
Putin handed out wounds around and accused Ankara of putting a knife in Moscow's back.
The scandal was all the greater because it came from an imagined friend.
Rebuilding true trust will be difficult, perhaps even impossible.
Russian public opinion has also changed since December.
State-controlled media has waged a massive nationwide campaign against Ankara for months.
All of a sudden it looked like the Turks were to blame for everything.
More serious were allegations from top state officials that Erdogan's own family benefited from an illegal oil trade in parts of Syria controlled by the so-called Islamic State.
He strongly denied this.
But in St. Petersburg came an official message that it was time to move on.
After all, this meeting happened only because Putin received an apology, which he demanded from President Erdogan.
Russia could also declare victory.
For Ankara, the benefits of declaring a ceasefire are clear.
In the first place, Erdogan was almost deprived of power last month during a failed coup and therefore needs all his friends.
Repeated terrorist attacks in Turkey visibly shook him, too.
There is also an economic motive.
Russia's sanctions have have had a harsh impact - in particular the abolition of charter flights, which carry several million Russian tourists each year on the coast of
This number decreased by almost 90%.
As far as Russia is concerned, tour operators and charter companies will certainly take a breather here when flights are eventually resumed.
They make money on Mediterranean tourism at the end of the season.
Even this week, state television promised cheaper fruits and vegetables when imports of agricultural commodities from Turkey were allowed again.
“Tourists there, tomatoes back here,” as the report proclaimed in the Vedemosti newspaper.
However, the visit had another political value for Moscow.
Ankara is angry with the West for what it sees as a feeble reaction to the coup attempt.
In addition, the long-lasting animosity of lengthy talks on joining the EU and Putin's entry - eager to allow the cooling to take advantage of and break Turkey's ties with the West.
The Russian leader certainly scored bonus points when Ankara asked him for the help of the elected authorities after attempting a coup.
You have to understand that for Moscow, which has a deep-rooted fear of regime change, it's given.
So the summit in this pompous, seaside palace allowed Russia and Turkey to demonstrate what one of the analysts described to me as "an alliance of the incompatible": how two countries that feel rejected and exploited by the West join forces.
However, despite publicly showing mutual reconciliation, there are fundamental differences between the two countries.
The first is Syria, where Moscow has not recently taken on the role of a peacemaker, but where Russia and Turkey stand on opposite sides.
It could be said that after nearly three-hour introductory talks, the two presidents said at a press conference that they had not even mentioned the issue.
Turkey's president deliberately avoided answering a question about the differences between them, while Putin decided to highlight those differences.
There is no clear deal under which the two states can find common ground on the issue of Syria.
But after months of open hostility - and given the risk of utter disaster, when a NATO member Turkey shot down a Russian warplane - it's certainly better that the two leaders at least talk to each other again.
Royal Bank of Scotland disappears for customers outside Scotland
According to the bank's chief executive, the RBS brand is to be suppressed into a back office role.
Royal Bank of Scotland is lost to customers outside of Scotland.
Ross McEwan told BBC Scotland that the RBS brand was linked to the bank's global ambitions.
Of them, she eased when, eight years ago, she almost disappeared and had to be redeemed from debt.
At the time, strategists were using "RBS" in order to protect other brands of consumer finance.
Millions of pounds spent on sponsoring international sport were behind it, from the Six Nations rugby Championship to Winbledom champion Andy Marray.
But it has now been decided that it is better to make the national brands stand out more.
The Royal Bank of Scotland will be used by Scottish customers, but will not launch new stores.
All references to RBS in England and Wales, with the exception of headquarters and stock coding on the stock exchange, will be changed to NatWest.
For customers in the Republic of Ireland and Northern Ireland, the Ulster Bank brand is already in use.
There are other, smaller brands for private banking that come more at the foreground - Coutts, Adam & Co, Drummond, and Holt's Military Bank.
During our trips to customers and employees at Inverness-shire, McEwan was responding to Ross McEwan's questions.
He told BBC Scotland: "RBS has ceased to be our investor brand and the brand that our employees work for, because we are now much more likely to become a bank of brands."
Because the bank itself has become a global brand, it has become both a global brand and RBS.
Now I'm saying we don't have global aspirations anymore, we've got efforts focused on the domestic scene.
Each of these brands will mean something completely different in their own communities, and our employees will work under these brands.
RBS has already stated that it will not continue its sponsorship of the Six Nations championship, has prepared a profile of individual brands of sponsorship of the sport.
“It’s a good time for us to move up to the brand bank, because the basic question (which we ask ourselves) is how to focus on becoming a better bank for customers?”
Three years ago, it would have been very cynical if we'd said we'd become a great bank for customers and introduced those brands.
But along with the way we've focused on the needs of our customers, and not our own, I think there's a lot of changes happening.
We can remind these brands again, I think the right time has come.
Frankie Dettori in Newmarket on horse Predilection led to the finish of the 3,3000th winner
