Russian President Putin has released the head of Sergei Ivanov's presidential office
Russian President Vladimir Putin unexpectedly released Sergei Ivanov from the post of head of the presidential office on Friday.
Ivanov has been one of Putin's closest collaborators for many years.
Sixty-three-year-old Ivanov has been appointed as special representative for ecology and transport.
The Kremlin's statement said Putin had "released Ivanov from his services to the head of Russia's presidential office," but there was no reason.
His place will be taken by Anton Vajno, who has been Ivanov's deputy since 2012.
Forty-four-year-old Vajno was previously a diplomat.
He was born in the capital of Estonia Tallinn in 1972, graduated at the prestigious State Institute of International Relations in Moscow (MGIMO) and worked at an embassy in Tokyo.
The website (in Russian) states that he later was the head of the presidential protocol and the presidential office.
In appointing Putin, he said, “Thank you for your trust.”
I believe that the most important role of the administration is to support your activities of the head of state within the meaning of drafting laws and controlling the extent to which your instructions are followed.
Putin told a Russian television station on Friday that Ivanov had asked him to be released from the post and recommended that he be replaced by Vajno.
Will he convince smiles?
This step completely mystified Moscow.
For a long time, Sergei Ivanov was one of Vladimir Putin's closest collaborators and, like Putin, worked for the KGB.
As head of the presidential office, he was one of the country's most powerful men.
At a meeting with President Putin, which aired on state television, the two men said they were leaving the post of head of the presidential office at their own request.
But smiles into the cameras convinced only a few people - especially now, just before parliamentary elections.
Is this the result of a struggle for power?
No one knows that yet.
But the official assertion that a man who was once thought of as a possible president is suddenly eager to lead Russia's environmental policies has been met with great disbelief.
The Kremlin’s website cites remarks addressed to Putin, in which Ivanov states: “It is true that in 2012 I asked you in an interview to confide in me this very demanding post, you could even call a problematic post, for four years.
It seems, I've been head of the presidential office for four years and eight months.
Ivanov took office in December 2011.
Previously, he held the position of Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Defence.
He is a member of the Russian Security Council and previously, like Putin, he worked for the State Security (KGB) committee.
At the end of the 1990s, when Putin commanded the Federal Security Service of the Russian Federation (FSB), which replaced the KGB, Ivanov was named his representative.
When Putin came to power, he appointed Ivanova as one of his five closest collaborators.
There has even been speculation that Ivanov could become Putin's successor at the head of state after his second term, because the third term would be unconstitutional for Putin.
However, this post was taken over by another Putin's close collaborator Dmitry Medvedev.
Putin became prime minister just three and a half years later before returning to the presidency.
