Queensferry Crossing: Bridge now connects with Fife
The 1.35 billion pound project is due to be completed by May 2017
The engineers connected the northern plateau and Queensferry Crossing and the viaduct - the bridge now connects with Fife.
Thus, it is the first of four closures between the “fenders of platforms”, which are now almost finished around each of the three pylons of the bridge.
Part of the bridge from Fife is now 600 meters long and weighs 30 000 tons.
There are 10 000 tons of iron and 20 000 tons of concrete and 46 hanging ropes.
The 1.35 billion pound project is due to be completed by May 2017.
Economic secretary Keith Brown visited the building today and was among the first to come from the mainland to the bridge.
He said: "This is a historical moment and a symbolic moment to build Queensferry Crossing.
On this project, we are all witnessing how construction engineers used more than 30 000 tons of concrete and steel to the structure of this part of the bridge on an impressive scale.
Despite the massive dimensions and weight of the bridge, the completion of the closure between the viaduct and the bridge platform was a delicate operation that required extraordinary accuracy.
There is now more than 79% of the total area of the bridge in place, concrete for the last parts of the platform is now poured in Rosyth, so the entire platform is ready to be lifted onto the bridge.
Michael Martin, head of the Forth Crossing Bridge Constructors project, said: "The first closure in the construction of every bridge is always an important milestone.
On this amazing project, this closure represents the top of building construction.
After installing the last part of the road platform and its connection to the North Tower expansion platform, we then had to close the last gap on the north side - towards the mainland.
We have done this by stretching the northern zoom viaduct 700 millimeters south.
This was a massive and at the same time very delicate operation.
Massive because the viaduct is 222 meters long, weighs about 6 000 tons and it was necessary to drag it with a climb of about 3%.
Delicate because the tolerance the team worked with were very small - only a few millimeters in each direction.
Luckily, everything went very well.
