A dream job for a hundred-thousand-dollar salary.
Well-paid work for which you don't even need college.
That's how it looks at first glance the work of an I.T. guy.
And often for the latter.
The advantage is not only an extremely high salary on Czech conditions (in Prague you earn up to 180 000 Kč by working in IT), but also extreme benefits.
This is due to a huge shortage of IT specialists.
Thousands of specialists arrived.
It is estimated that almost 20 000 IT specialists are missing in the Czech Republic.
According to the statistics of the Grafton recruitment agency, programmers, developers, network engineers and application specialists are among the most wanted.
Candidates can choose between several job offers.
Therefore, candidates do not compete for a position, but jobs for candidates, comments Jitka Součková, marketing manager Grafton Recruitment.
College is not a necessity.
Higher education is not a necessity for IT professionals.
Higher education means a good foundation and is an overall indicator of a candidate's intelligence and educability, but it often doesn't have much to do with knowledge of development.
Developers have to constantly learn new things, which will not be replaced by a graduated university, says Petr Kubačka, director of Monster Career CZ.
You make the most money in Prague.
The wages of I.T. guys are everywhere far above the national average.
Most of the money will come in Prague and Brno, it is no longer such a chart in the regions.
"Wage conditions in Brno are comparable to those in Prague," Kubačka confirms.
Technical directors will reach the highest earnings.
In Prague and Brno they can come to 120 000 - 180 000 Kč.
In other regions the salary is lower, but it does not fall below 80 000 Kč.
IT project managers are also in demand.
On arrival they can earn 60 000 - 100 000 Kč.
There's also a fight over graduates.
While in some professions graduates often have to go straight to the employment office, this is not the case with I.TMs.
If companies drag themselves and overpay an expert from another company, they will gladly reach for a graduate.
They can raise it according to themselves.
They can't do without language.
The knowledge of a foreign language is useful in every profession, but at least decent English is necessary for I.T
Knowing at least one foreign language is a necessity for some IT positions.
On the other hand, we encounter a number of cases when companies are mainly looking for a professionally educated and experienced person and are satisfied only with the average knowledge of English.
However, if, for example, a support worker speaks multiple foreign languages, it is interesting for his employer because he can serve more customers from different countries, adds Director Kubačka.
Yeah at Google, there's paradise.
Only quietly envious Czech workers can envy employees at Google.
Of course there are flexible working hours, the possibility of working from home or various part-times, not only for mothers.
"The company is giving new dads four extra weeks of vacation after the birth of their baby," says Martina Joneková, a Google spokeswoman.
Pamperation also includes health care for employees and family, meals, transportation, training, wellness or massage directly at the workplace.
Whoever wants to be educated has the grend and it doesn't matter what's new to learn.
But the benefits don’t end there.
Such a full refrigerator for free is also definitely not to be thrown away.
A healthy lunch (in some locations as well as breakfast and dinner) is available on site every day.
In addition, there are so-called microkitchens in all offices, where people always find a healthy snack, the spokesperson reveals.
And so that nothing has to be thrown away, so what doesn't eat, he puts in boxes that employees can take home in the evening.
And the reason for these unbelievable advantages in the Czech Republic?
To create a creative and motivating environment!
“It’s not important for Google for employees to sit in the office for eight hours a day, but the results of initiatives or projects, shift or development, ideas and innovative solutions.”
The U.N. sharply criticized Bulgaria for criminalizing refugees.
Zeid Ra'ad al-Hussein, the U.N
The Bulgarian government's official strategy "causes serious concern that Bulgaria does not comply with international law," Zeid Ra'ad al-Hussein warned in a statement.
"It is particularly disturbing to witness how important and influential public figures publicly express their support for criminal armed militias, which are brazenly organizing hunts for refugees on the border between Bulgaria and Turkey."
"One of the most serious problems is that almost all people who switch to Bulgarian territory in an irregular way are automatically arrested," Zeid stressed.
And even worse - they're often prosecuted and imprisoned - for a year or more - if they try to leave the country.
Their attempts to leave the country are criminalized despite everyone having the right under international law to leave any country, including their own.
Bulgaria arrested 14 000 migrants this year, last year for the same period 21 000.
Pokémon Go - the pursuit of health and happiness that pays off
It's fascinating to see how the world is changing.
Over the past decade, many people have lamented that teenagers and children no longer play outdoors and prefer to spend their free times over screens.
Pokémon Go lifts people off sofas and leads them to activity.
The time spent in front of screens originally shrunk to TV, later to video games and, most recently, to smartphones.
It's great to see that technology and activities outdoors don't excrete each other.
I am talking, of course, about fashion, which is Pokémon Go, an application based on characters that first appeared in video games in the late 1990s (later a cartoon series, playing cards and more).
As with many cultural phenomena, there was a decrease in interest before the launch of this new game at the beginning of July in the USA, Australia and New Zealand.
The launch in other countries was then postponed due to the massive interest that led to server overload.
Pokémon Go gained more users than Tinder in a matter of days and nearly as many users as Twitter.
How does it work?
In short, players on a smartphone go out looking for Pokémons that track using GPS, and when they find them, they can “picture” them (via a screenshot on your phone) or “catch”.
Mania has engulfed the generation that played the game at a younger age, lifts it from its seats and drives it out to look for Pokémon.
In the U.S., thanks to this game, there has been a significant increase in the number of steps that an “average” person does every day.
Cardiogram for smartwatches from Apple, which tracks 35,000 users, recently told the Washington Post that within two days since the launch of the game, the number of tracked people who exercised every day for 30 minutes has jumped from 45 to 53 percent.
The company doesn't have information about who Pokémon Go plays - it only sees crude data about exercise levels.
Many public campaigns aimed at “finding 30 people”, “get up from the couch” and “active every day” have fallen on barren ground, because most public-healthy messages are boring and designed for people who don’t look good.
Nevertheless, the importance of exercise must not be underestimated.
Those who exercise regularly reduce the risk of cancer or depression.
Physical activity contributes to weight control, high blood pressure, diabetes and a long list of benefits that we can't give here.
But the beauty of Pokémon Go is that people go out and do something they enjoy.
Thus you cover three pillars of health - movement, fresh air and the sun's rays. And fun.
Some people even make new friends in pursuit of Pokémon.
Of course, there are also such boring patrons who are looking for any excuse to complain.
And yes, someone can walk into the door.
And what, like?
There's been a breakthrough.
In their time, smartphone apps, such as Fitbit, could track our activity, but they didn't give us a reason to do something, as is the case with this game.
The novelty catches up and the interest in it begins to decline at some point.
However, thanks to the success of Pokémon, there will be other games that will combine apps and virtual reality with activity in the real world and will help a number of people in the pursuit of activity, health and a better form.
Read Dr. Joe's blog at www.drjoetoday.com
