Police: Three casualties in serial killer case shot while listening to music in car
According to a police report released on Thursday, two women and a 12-year-old girl died during the most serious assault of the decade, in the case of Phoenix's first serial killer. They were shot while sitting in a car, chatting and listening to music.
Authorities reported that Angela Rochelle Liner, Stefanie R. Ellis and Ellis Maleah's daughter were shot on June 12 in a car parked in the driveway.
The report said the armed man stood on the passenger side and fired eight shots from his handgun. He then escaped in another vehicle.
According to police, the robbery is not behind the triple murder, because the attacker did not take the wallets, nor the amount of 2,900 dollars that one of the adult victims had with him.
Police spokesman in Phoenix, Sgt. Jonathan Howard, said investigators are looking into whether the shooting had any connection to any of the previous four attacks.
In two mostly Hispanic neighborhoods, a total of nine attacks occurred within four months in which seven people were killed and two others wounded.
“It’s happening in a residential area in front of the houses,” Howard said earlier this month for KPHO’s CBS office.
Police believe the suspect used several cars, including a brown Nissan from the late 1990s, a black BMW from the late 90's and a white Cadillac or Lincoln.
Investigators declined to divulge what evidence led them to conclude that the attacks were related to each other and were carried out by the same killer.
Ellis (33) was taken to hospital, where she later succumbed.
Liner (31) and Ellis Maleah's daughter died at the scene.
A witness told police it wasn't uncommon for women to sit in a parked car and listen to music.
Authorities also released a police report about the shooting, the target of which became an empty van just half an hour before the three-fold murder. Police believe its originator was this serial killer.
Investigators found shell casings and assembled a bullet from the remains of bullets in the seat and from under the hood of the engine.
Six-year-old mother with microcephaly: ‘His condition improves’
Edmund Picciuto, like most children his age, loves singing, toys and, of course, his mum.
But unlike his peers, he suffers from microcephaly, which is the result of a genetic disorder that his mother, Elizabeth Picciuto, did not know about during her pregnancy.
When he first got home, I was the first to think I couldn't do this.
I can't do this.
I'm not ready for this.
Which isn't true at all - of course you're ready for it," she said.
She and her husband, Vincent, learned that Edmund might never recognize them and that she might not even survive.
Some doctors even suggested that he be placed in an institution.
“My first reaction was that I didn’t think people were doing this anymore,” Picciut said.
Edmund walks with the help of a walker.
Until the age of three, he couldn't sit or crawl, and he still doesn't talk.
However, some milestones are slowly being fulfilled.
He walks with help and rides his bike.
He also likes to play with his two brothers and loves singing.
Elizabeth demonstrates sign language through which Edmund says “please sing to me.”
“We’re tauring him sign language,” Picciut told CBS News.
But it's not common sign language - Edmund says he invented some characters himself.
Microcephaly can arise from a number of different conditions: genetic, such as Edmund, and after infections in pregnancy caused by viruses such as measles or Zika.
Symptoms and prognosis may differ substantially.
It's too early for us to be able to say what impact Zika's virus will have on the lives of those infected.
Picciute recalls an emotional toll levied by an insecure future.
I keep saying, “What’s next?”
What's next?
And she's still not sure about it.
In fact, I've never asked what the overall prognosis is because I don't expect anyone to be able to tell me at all.
Picciute, however, has an encouraging message for the mothers of all children who suffer from congenital defects caused by the Zika virus.
The situation is improving.
Know it'll be better.
You will love your child and your child will love you.
Heavy rains and flooding required rescue efforts in Louisiana, Mississippi
Heavy rain and widespread flooding in Louisiana prompted the governor to declare a state of emergency on Friday. More rains are anticipated in the state on Saturday.
