A cure for sixty crowns a day can reduce the risk of a heart attack
Thousands of patients at risk of heart attack will be able to take a drug that costs about sixty crowns (two pounds). Authorities recommend administering it to more patients for a longer period of time.
Ticagrelor's anti-clotting drug reduces the risk of having a seizure again in people with heart disease.
The drug is now given for 12 months after having a heart attack to reduce the risk of stroke or any other seizure.
NHS watchdog NICE recommends that the drug be given to people for four years in order to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
However, NHS dog watch NICE recommends that it be given to people for four years in order to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
In England, about 140 000 people suffer a heart attack every year, and a quarter of them suffer another seizure or stroke.
Heart attack and strokes are caused by the build-up of fat, which forms a continuous layer on the walls of the arteries.
If this layer breaks off, a blood clot may develop, block blood access to the heart and develop a heart attack.
If the clot relaxes, it can continue through the bloodstream, block the blood supply to the brain and cause a stroke.
People who have already had a heart attack are at a higher risk of having another seizure.
Ticagrelor, which is produced by AstraZeneca and which is sold under the brand name Brilique, reduces the risk by making clots less likely.
It is recommended that patients receive 90 mg of ticagrelor for 12 months and 60 mg together with aspirin, twice a day for another three years.
Professor Carole Longson, NICE's Director of Technology Evaluation, said: "Despite the availability of effective secondary preventive treatment, there are a quarter of patients who have had a heart attack and who will suffer another heart attack or stroke - often with devastating consequences."
Fear of repetition can have a significant negative impact on the patient's quality of life.
Ticagrelor, in combination with aspirin, has been shown to be effective in reducing the dangers of further heart attacks and strokes in people who have already suffered a heart attack.
We are pleased to be able to expand the treatment options available to many thousands of people who will be able to benefit from it.
Since information about the efficacy and safety of Ticagrelor - primarily about the dangers of bleeding - beyond three years is limited, treatment beyond this time is not recommended in the design.
