According to my conversations, people got rid of their collections most often between 1998 and 1999.
Customers who once sold their turntable records and bought CDs are now selling their CDS to buy their gramophone records back.”
This could explain the boom of lovers of gramophone records of a certain age.
But what about the supposed emotional importance of turntable records?
It doesn’t take you more than a few seconds to find a pleasant community of people with similar mindsets in a shop like Phonica.
According to Wez, some older customers are particularly scandalized by a system of non-contact payments.
Some fans of gramophone records may feel overwhelmed in the digital world, opting for the well-known environment of gramophone record covers, things that evoke memories of peaceful adolescence.
“I think filling a gap or owning something material can bring some comfort,” Wez says.
I'm a collector myself, that's the way it is for me, and I think with a lot of people too.
