A court in France has refused to demolish shops in the Jungle refugee camp
A court in France has dismissed an order by authorities in Calais to demolish dozens of makeshift shops and restaurants in the Jungle camp where thousands of refugees live.
During a court hearing this week, authorities in Calais argued that the state should be able to cancel a total of 72 makeshift shops, where people inside the camp sell goods, from cigarettes to energy drinks, offer cutting hair, in makeshift restaurants selling tea for 50 cents or basic dishes for several euros.
The prefect's office in Calais argued that places need to be demolished because they represent a parallel economy that doesn't pay taxes, and because they pose a risk of fire and health risks.
Humanitarian organizations said in court that shops and restaurants were vital and that free meals offered by state-backed associations and other groups did not provide a food supplement for the growing numbers of refugees in the camp.
Disputed makeshift shops and restaurants often provide shelter and free food to people in need.
The Lille court ruled that there was no legal basis that prevented people from running 72 makeshift shops, but said the objections of Prefect Calais were "completely understandable."
Judge Jean-François Molla said grocery stores, cafes and restaurants have a much bigger role than simply serving food to people who "live in extraordinarily desperate conditions."
He said the venues provide quiet places to meet refugees and volunteers.
One of the places threatened with demolition was a cafe in the camp, Jungle Book Kids, which is operated by a non-profile organization, which offers 200 meals daily, hours of English and French and asylum counseling for hundreds of unaccompanied minors in the camp.
More than 170 000 people have signed a petition to protect the “child” café.
The Guardian has received a letter saying that closing the café “was a disaster for these children, some of them are only 8 years old.”
On Friday, Help Refuges and L'Auberge des Migrants estimated that the number of refugees in the camp, after a steep increase in arrivals during the summer, has risen to more than 9,000 - which is still the most in all time.
