Big brother's here, here's the first surveillance SIM coming.
You won't escape her.
The new rules should apply to all persons without a Thai passport, who decide to purchase a SIM of one of the local operators when staying in Thailand.
That is, not only for tourists, but also for people with long-term visas who, for example, work in Thailand or marry there.
The secretary-general of the National Broadcasting and Telecommunications Commission, Takorn Tantasith, said there would be no exceptions.
The SIM sale plan intended exclusively for foreigners was presented by the national telecommunications regulator in early August.
The new measure, which will allow their location to be tracked, is intended to be implemented by authorities within six months.
According to Tantasith, it is a means to preserve national security that will make it possible to deal with crimes committed by foreigners.
In addition, the so-called tourist SIM should also help to detect people who exceed the allowed length of stay.
In the case of retailers, a special SIM for foreigners should also appear alongside SIM intended exclusively for Thais within six months.
A difference will be the tracking function, which will be permanently switched on for cards intended for people without a Thai passport.
It won't be able to be switched off.
"This feature is not with SIMs for Thais, because we can always easily track them," Tantasith added.
It should be added that in Thailand, subscriptions are not anonymous as in the Czech Republic.
The seller requires proof of identity.
On the other hand, for several years one of the operators handed out a free starter SIM to tourists at airports.
They were completely anonymous.
Ten telecoms regulators from other Asian countries, including Malaysia and Singapore, are also considering introducing the same measure as Thailand.
There is currently a mandatory SIM registration, but it is not possible to use the tracking system legally.
However, the upcoming measure does not imply an obligation for foreigners to purchase a local operator’s SIM when entering the country.
Of course, they can use roaming services with their own home SIM, in which case the Thai authorities will be short on them.
Operators of a given country know only the relevant network information after crossing the border, i.e. after logging a SIM to their network.
Therefore, they know the phone number, but not information about the owner.
However, the difference in roaming prices compared to the rates of local operators is so abysmal that even the majority of tourists start looking for local SIMs during their stay in Thailand.
Authorities' efforts to oversee the movement of foreigners legally received immediate criticism from foreigners living in Thailand.
Tantasith reassures the situation and in connection with the protection of privacy compared the upcoming system to the obligation to provide a residential address in immigration documents.
In addition, the police will need an injunction if necessary to monitor a specific person.
There will be an unspecified punishment for any abuse of the system.
"If the service provider monitors the location of the SIM without an injunction, it will be charged with a crime," Tantasith added.
In addition, the system should maximise the use of prepaid cards.
While a specific number is currently recycled only after the 90-day period, after which the SIM did not subscribe to the network, the number could now be restored after fifteen days in the event of leaving Thailand.
Luxury apartment in a new building in Vinohrady has a swimming pool and a whirlpool on the terrace.
Why did you choose an apartment in a new building?
We were looking for a carefree apartment, where most of the care of the property will be provided by the administrator.
The condition was plenty of parking spaces and an elevator in the house, which essentially disqualified the older buildings.
Not to mention problematic acoustics in old houses, problems with floor swinging, high energy intensity and the like.
Large terraces were also a condition, which the old development is not able to offer.
Other interesting visits can be found in the magazine Modern apartment.
The entire interior was designed for you by architect Lenka Langerová with her colleague Kamila Poláková from Artiga studio.
Why did you address them?
Would you recommend working with an expert to everyone?
We would recommend cooperation with the architect to anyone, if he designs an apartment from the beginning, because it is a very complex technical process.
The second level is the interior design itself: designers have a significantly better idea of the range of possible materials and, based on the wishes of the client, are able to match the interior appropriately.
We cooperated with the architect Lenka Langerová already on our mountain apartment, then on the basis of the personal recommendation of another client, and since everything worked well, it was an obvious choice for us even during the reconstruction of our new apartment in Vinohrady.
What were your practical and aesthetic requirements?
We wanted a generous social and residential space with adequate facilities for a family of four conceived as a modern, but not cold interior.
The priority was to achieve the interconnection of the apartment with terraces and to realize the intelligent control of the household.
And, of course, it was necessary to monitor the quality of execution.
Did you leave everything to the experts, or did you interfere with the final form of the interior yourself?
We approved all designs, including technical drawings and samples of materials, but the design always came from architects.
At the beginning, we set what we expected from the design, and showed some elements of the interior, which we would like to incorporate into the design, for example carpets or paintings.
I think it is our collection of mostly Czech authors involving works by Karel Malich, Karel Vysušil, Václav Špála or Otto Gutfreund that helped the architect to navigate our taste.
Also, the combination of black, white and walnut wood was the architect's idea as well as individual pieces of furniture, walls, floors, lighting, carpets, textiles, art, etc. We then verified together through visualizations.
Have there been any complications during the implementation, due to which you had to give up the original ideas, or, on the contrary, accept new ones?
Perhaps only in the initial phase of the project, when we came to the construction at the moment when it was first possible to ascend to the height of our floor, and found that the views we were planning actually shade the surrounding buildings.
That's when we literally changed the disposition overnight from the ground up.
Granite was used on the kitchen line as well as on the lining of the fireplace wall.
In the interior, wooden surfaces, complemented by leather, for example on the upholstery of dining and bar chairs Norma from Arper, predominate.
How would you characterize the interior?
Are you planning any changes here or is there anything you would like to have differently today?
I believe that it is a modern airy interior, which primarily fulfills its purpose.
We are not planning changes, most of the modifications after moving took place on the level of shielding and settlement of terraces.
Perhaps the only rebuke is to the floor, when walnut wood turns out to be too sensitive to a household with a dog.
Word architect, Ing. arch. Lenka Langerová and MgA. Kamila Hornychová, author of the realization.
The apartment is on the last floor of the new building, so it was possible to carry out a completely new layout and several other modifications.
For the interior, an important element is the placement of the sun tunnels in the ceiling of the hallway and the main toilet.
In doing so, we got daylight into the darker spaces of the apartment.
Also, the direct entrance from the main bathroom to the roof terrace to the pool from USSPA is one of the building modifications, which was very beneficial for the function of the entire apartment.
There is also a swimming pool and a hot tub.
Compared to the French, you are not negative, says a Muslim woman living in Prague.
You're from France.
Where did you live before you moved to Prague?
My parents are originally from Algeria.
I was born in the south of France, near the Spanish border.
Quite I've been moving.
I've lived in Toulouse, Lyon and Marseille.
I studied journalism in France, so I got to work in a magazine.
So why did you decide to move out of the country?
I was beginning to feel like I was suffocating in France.
I just didn't want to live there anymore.
Mainly because of the atmosphere among people.
It seemed to me that every Frencher is terribly negative.
People in France are negatively attuned in general, but it's gotten even worse lately.
It's interesting that you're mentioning negativity.
Some people evaluate Czech nature in this way.
Believe me, you're no negative compared to the French.
It may not happen to some people when they come to France, because they don't see it as much as tourists.
But it was too much for me.
The big theme is money.
People feel that they have little money, while the minimum wage in France is fifteen hundred euros, which I think is enough.
Now there's also a problem with people's behavior towards Muslims.
That's completely incomprehensible to me.
For example, in the street I lived in, my non-Muslim neighbor started looking at me just because I'm Muslim.
It's like I'm evil.
That's why I didn't feel well in France.
But my family stayed there.
So you've decided to go to another country.
Why did you choose the Czech Republic?
I was looking for a job basically anywhere else and I came across a vacancy with Air France here in Prague.
Admittedly, I didn't know Prague at first.
I knew something about the Czech Republic, my father even told me about what was happening in Czechoslovakia, but I didn’t even know what to expect from Prague.
I've been here for three years now, and I can't have imagined I wouldn't live here.
Was it a big difference when you moved from France to Prague?
Mainly because of a completely different atmosphere.
Maybe it's because I don't speak Czech, so I can't capture some negative reactions or curses, but I feel like people here are freer.
That I can do anything, dress in any way, and no one will judge me.
In France, you're always looking at unpleasantly, for example, when you're wearing special clothes and stuff like that.
I started to feel really free in Prague.
I don't think Praguers care about things like that.
Where do you work now?
After working at Air France, I worked for one travel website for a while.
Afterwards, my friend and I started a website on which people offer and are looking for shared housing.
So far, we only operate in Prague, but we would like to extend it to the whole of Central Europe.
Do you have any Czech friends in Prague?
I have here two very close friends, who are halved by Czechs, and two more Czech friends.
I have to say that another specificity of the Czechs is that at first they keep their distance more.
He won't let someone near his body.
That's quite different from the place where I grew up.
In the south, they're all hugging and kissing on the cheek.
It's different here, but I understand it's a cultural issue, so I'm trying to adapt.
You mentioned that you don't speak Czech.
Is the language barrier a problem for you?
On the contrary, it is quite simple in Prague, because perhaps everyone here speaks English.
Even in curfews or food.
I'd like to learn Czech, but I don't have so much time.
And by being able to communicate at least a little bit in English, I don’t have the need to learn the language quickly.
And every time I have a problem, there's someone to help me.
Have you encountered any negative reactions?
Maybe because you're Muslim?
Basically, no.
Maybe it's also because I'm working in an international environment that's more tolerant.
There's one experience I can think of.
My manager, a Czech woman, once told me about the French football team and said something in the sense: It's not French, it's Africans.
That touched me quite a bit.
But that someone had a problem with being a Muslim has never happened to me yet.
That's even though a colleague once alerted me to some anti-Muslim demonstrations.
He told me not to go downtown, that they'd scold me.
Anti-Muslim demonstrations are partly related to terrorist attacks.
How does the current situation affect you?
Are you afraid, for example, that something similar can happen in Paris, Nice or Brussels?
I feel safe in Prague.
But the problem is that anything can happen anywhere these days.
Until recently, I couldn't have imagined anything like this happening in France.
And it's not just about Muslims.
Moreover, Islam does not recognize violence at all.
For me, people who kill are not Muslims.
The Qur'an says that man has no right to kill because he is not God.
How do you see this when terrorists shield themselves from your faith?
It's very hard.
I feel shame and guilt, even though I shouldn't feel that way at all.
I don't try to keep track of the violence, but every time I read something, I need to apologize for something that isn't really my fault.
I'm a completely different person, so why should I apologise for terrorists?
We met at Jiřího z Poděbrady Square.
On Wednesday, Czech Muslims, together with Christians, demonstrated against violence.
Have you registered it?
I didn't know about that.
But I understand that these people feel the need to prove that they don't agree with the violence.
I don't think they have to do anything like that.
Why should they feel guilty for something they didn't cause?
Do you meet other Muslims in Prague?
My collaborators, for example, are from Egypt and other Muslim countries.
They're a little more religious than I am, so sometimes we have quite wild discussions about Islam, because we all perceive it a little differently.
I come from France, so I went through a different education, they learned Islam in school.
We have different approaches, but we respect each other.
People sometimes associate Islam with the subordinate status of a woman.
How do you view it?
There's no way I'm subordinate.
Rather the opposite.
I'm a woman, I'm strong.
It also has to do with the fact that you don't carry any headgear.
Hijab, for example?
And it's mostly the way I've been educated.
My mother, for example, is a truly principled Muslim.
He prays five times a day, but doesn't wear the scarf.
She's always taught me that faith is purely personal.
It depends on everyone how they interpret it.
When you read the Quran, you should make up one's own opinion about those things.
So when I read a passage in the Quran that says that God told the Prophet to hide his wives from the eyes of others, I said to myself, “I’m not a Prophet’s wife, so why should I hide?”
On the other hand, I respect that, and when I come, for example, to Iran or Saudi Arabia, where they require headscarves, I take it.
Are you planning to stay in Prague?
So far, yes.
I didn't feel so well anywhere else.
I've been thinking about Poland for a while, but I'm doing great in Prague.
I can even imagine raising my own children here.
In my opinion, Prague is an ideal city.
Czech Muslims, together with Christians, demonstrated against violence.
Yachtar Kozelská Fenclová wind in Rio took the advancing rungs.
Czech yachtswoman Veronica Kozelská Fenclová made her Friday rides at sea in Rio de Janiero, Brazil.
In the Laser Radial class, she moved up to 10th place before the final two races.
This would ensure the Czech to participate in the final ride.
You'll collect the packages like Pokémon.
They want to compete with carriers.
TV director Matěj Liška and the manager of the Prague music club Marek Greger are behind the idea of using already planned trips to transport shipments.
With the idea, they breathed life into three applications with a different geographical reach.
We launched the national application Transfer in May.
At the moment there are thousands of cities recorded in it and we will expand the network.
In the Czech Republic, we first want to learn this before we focus more on abroad, the forty-one-year-old manager says in an interview with iDNES.cz.
How does the transfer application work and who can benefit from it?
You can download it and register for free.
Then you can enter the route and the data, how big the package you can transport and how much you want.
Maybe someone's looking for a transfer that day.
When both the route and the time of delivery are suitable, it is paired and both receive mutual notification.
At that moment, a cottage opens, where the pair arrange a handover.
How many people do you think would have to register in order for each package to always find their courier?
I don't know the ideal state, I can't estimate how many registered and how many paths it takes to make the coverage completely optimal and every offer paired with demand.
It's already working on the main routes, and it's logically easier than, for example, on the road between villages.
The more people, the greater the potential for speed and accuracy.
In the meantime, people are also building up confidence and finding out that no one escapes with the package.
Haven't you haven to deal with such a
How many users have the service two months after launch?
At the moment we have about 5 to 6 thousand people who create paths.
We consider this a success, and we believe there will be others packed.
Isn't there a danger that the sender will wait too long?
We hope that we will be competing for large conglomerates only because we are able to deliver the thing the same day and for a fraction of the price.
With a large company for delivery within two hours, you will pay several thousand.
Here you pay maybe a hundred and less, because that's a path that someone is already taking.
Should traditional carriers be worried about the potential loss of customers in the future?
We're hoping that's the case.
The market has huge potential, feeding on huge multinational carriers here, as well as retail chains, where everyone started shopping and gradually discovered that the quality does not correspond to what it pays for.
No one wants to find a note in the mailbox with a message that he should pick up the shipment at the branch, first that day after 19 o'clock.
At that time I needed the package for six hours and I was waiting at home, because it was important.
We want a better, faster and functional service that won't annoy anyone and send a signal to the competition to try.
Marek Greger (41) and Matěj Liška (39).
Not one has ever worked in information technology.
Marek Greger works as the manager of the music club, Matěj Liška as a television director.
In 2014, they began preparing the Shippansee LTD application, which uses already-planned journeys between the world's capitals, primarily aircraft, to transport packages.
In 2015, they commissioned a project for the processing of a company that created applications, for example for MALL.cz, Jobs.cz or ČSFD.
The programming lasted for three quarters of a year and the application was launched in March 2016.
They started to develop the transfer application in December last year and were released in May this year.
It is a local version of Shippansee adapted for the Czech market.
Mesík and Mesík Profi have been developed since January and made available to users at the end of July 2016.
The intention from the beginning was to do all of these applications, which one day merge into one.
In the Czech Republic, they want to fine-tune the application and then, in cooperation with a strong investor, focus more on the global market.
The sharing economy has been a topic lately, and this is an AirBnB or Uber service.
Aren't you worried about operating in an area that no one can tell much about yet, where is the sharing line and where the grey economy is?
It's the same, it's just a different product.
It's clear to us that if it succeeds and we go up with it, we'll be the next thorn in the eye through the grey economy.
But even though someone saves and someone earns, there's no intention of bypassing tax authorities and tax controls.
Each user, whether on one side or the other, will be urged to tax their incomes if they make income out of it.
How do you think the service has so much potential?
The sender saves and earns, respectively, reduces the cost of the trip.
This is a strong idea that unites interest on both sides.
In the time of social media, which divides people, we would like this on the contrary to converge.
Nice stories can arise, people are delighted that they have helped someone and will talk about it.
Maybe someone'll bring you forgotten glasses, keys or pills.
In my opinion, people have been missing a personal approach for a long time, we say about this app that it's people's service.
So how do you guarantee that the courier won't flee?
Each user of the application, together with registration, scans his/her/her/her/her/her/her/her/her/her/her/her/her
These are channels that give credibility to both the service and man.
Couriers are also collecting asterisks based on sensor ratings.
We will also offer insurance, we are still tuning the details.
The courier is liable for the uninsured consignment in the event of loss or damage, but does not take responsibility for the content under criminal law if there is something illegal and does not know about it.
If the police stop him and he finds a submachine gun or ten kilos of hashish in a sealed package in his suitcase, criminal responsibility falls to the sender.
Isn't there a risk of misusing sensitive data from the database?
Registered are, if necessary, traceable by the administrative authority, which will be the only one who will have the opportunity to inspect it, even we ourselves are not able to see them, the data is protected against misuse.
How much money will the courier receive after the successful delivery of the shipment?
The courier determines the price and receives the full amount he asks for.
The sender is charged several percent extra for mediating contact.
This money is our profit, but it's only a few percent, because we want to push for volume.
The courier can also not be blamed that he did not deliver the shipment when he actually delivered it, receipt and delivery is verified using QR codes.
Are there restrictions?
Whether in relation to the size or content of the package?
You can transport anything.
We've got three sizes, as small as a cube of butter, medium as a plate of eggs and as big as bass of beers.
Then there is no further restriction, and if someone has a place in the car, for example, transports the refrigerator.
Plus, the sender has the option to take a picture of the package in the app, so the courier can see what's being asked.
You don’t even have to pack the thing if you don’t want to hide content.
You put the forgotten sweatshirt in the courier's hand and that's it.
Who came up with the idea and how long did it take to run the service?
My friend got the idea more than two years ago, I didn't pay much attention to it back then.
He then showed me a demo application with a transnational reach, which mainly targets passengers by plane.
At that moment, I told him I wasn't going to look for an investor, but I'd like to become one myself and put savings into it.
It took another year's development before we founded Shippansee LTD in London due to the greater credibility of the entire product.
However, this is not the version you are focusing on right now.
No, we focused marketing on the Czech Republic, where we are trying to capture the application, because it all had labor pains and there is always something to deal with and improve.
We are trying to respond to customer feedback and we do not solve the foreign market so much at the moment.
We know it's a long-haul run, but we're nursing, we want to create a really good product for people.
Shippansee LTD was the basic and most expensive application, the other two were upgraded in a way.
The transfer arose when we said that the national segment can be much more interesting and that we will at least learn it in the Czech Republic.
The thirst application Mesíkem is local, for the city.
However, it can send a package to the exact address to the other side of the world.
It works for the sender in the same way as Transfer, but the difference is that as a courier you see shipments in the form of points on the map that are closest to you, thanks to the geolocator in your mobile.
The application for couriers who want to make a living with it, we have called Mesík Profi.
So someone can make extra money by picking up packages around the city?
That's right, the last app in Prague will be downloaded by a taxi driver, a student, a mother on parental leave, someone who wants to make his way to work.
Once everyone's fooling around with these Pokémons right now, they can collect this the same.
You can see where there are orders around the city, come to it, click and see when it has to be delivered and transport the shipment.
It's set up cheaper than competitive services.
At the moment, we are still doing a web interface, which will be crucial for companies to send shipments, fill in data about them on the website, i.e. create points on the map.
We are then able to invoice their use of the service every month.
Are you now a full-time project?
It makes us, but we have it normally at work so that we can support the project and develop it.
There is no strong multinational investor behind us, we are two boys, one of whom had a brilliant idea and the other jumped into it with other suggestions and ideas.
Now we are looking for an investor strong enough to manage not the Czech market with us, but who should, by pardone the ball, go with us to a worldwide product and become another huge player on the market like AirBnB and Uber.
Titanfall 2 is supposed to bring almost forgotten variety.
This will fix the second installment, and the solo game in it, according to the first information, will be more varied than in Call of Duty.
The length will be around the standard eight hours and the content should be based on the concept of the network struggle in the first part.
Therefore, you can look forward to freedom of movement, which, in addition to running, also includes the possibility of using a fighting titan.
In addition to the action passages, the game will include levels focused precisely on skillful movement.
They'll commemorate platformers and include puzzles.
Therefore, for example, double jumps, running on walls and rope slips are essential to overcome them.
In addition, sometimes it will be necessary to eliminate enemies.
There will also be maps reminiscent of mazes and levels with special concepts.
In one, for example, it will be possible to lift and landslide platforms and close massive doors.
You will modify the shape of the battlefield.
Thanks to this, the pace of the game should oscillate between furious action and calmer moments to explore an unknown planet.
We're going to play for pawn Jack Cooper dreaming of a career as a pilot.
At the beginning of the campaign, Cooper, along with Captain Lastimosa, crashes on the alien planet Typhon inhabited by enemy soldiers.
There will be non-levial to him, not even the wilderness there.
In addition, Lastimosa suffers fatal wounds during the crash, and Cooper is tasked with commissioning the captain's titan.
His dream of a pilot's career will come true sooner than he expected.
However, the first step will be to steal the battery necessary for the robot's repair.
There'll also become Cooper's calls with the titan.
It will serve as a source of information about the soldier's past, but at the same time, humorous moments will be created by robotic depersonalized communication.
For example, if titanium lists dryly all the injuries Cooper is at risk during the planned jump.
The first information about the campaign in Titanfall 2 was provided by Polygon.
Moreover, it will be possible to assess the result in two months, and Bonusweb will bring you the first impressions of the network game after Gamescom.
Titanfall 2 will be released on October 28 for PC, PlayStation 4 and Xbox One.
