Thirty years ago, while I was retiring, my wife, after thirty years of marriage, found a twenty-year-old husband, our neighbor.
I knew it from the beginning - at first she obviously tried for it for a long time, then they got together.
She's such a big guy, probably her type, because she used to walk with the like in front of me.
She's gone completely crazy about him.
In those first few days, her voice even changed, she had such a girl's, loving voice.
But when I told her I knew what was going on, she denied it.
But I watched the whole thing.
He used to come home from work regularly at half past four, so she always went shopping at three.
She tried every day to at least see him.
Later I found out that they were meeting in his house, in the laundry room.
How many times did I know they were there.
I got the keys, he was determined to catch them there.
But I don't know why, in the end I've always sat at home.
I've never been able to go there, take that decisive step.
You may laugh at me, but I believe my wife thoughtfully controlled me.
I think she soon found out that I'm controllable that I'm going to do whatever she wants.
And I actually did it.
Maybe I'm just such a slapper.
At the time, the neighbors told me that they knew about it too.
He was such a jerk that I never asked them what to do.
I was afraid that if it all cracked, it'd be bad.
Only the daughter suspected that it would affect everyone in the family.
So I've always put it off, on and on.
One time, however, I went to his apartment, that I needed help with something in the laundry room.
He went with me and I told him that I got anonymous, that he was meeting with my wife and that he was suggesting everything.
I asked him what he was going to tell me.
He stood there, shrugging his shoulders, staring at the ground, not saying a word.
Then, at that time, I also met his wife on the way out of the store.
I told her, but she didn't believe it at all.
She didn't react to it and didn't tell me anything either.
No one said anything to me then.
I'm the kind of person who's never gone anywhere much, who's having trouble meeting someone, let alone finding a woman.
So I didn't even think about divorce.
I kept relying on the fact that it would end one day.
I figured I'd forgive her for it all, and life would move on.
But that wasn't the case.
And I've basically never dealt with all of this.
It's still in me to this day.
I've shown it to her quite often, but in the end I've left it easy.
He's still living in the house next door, and I'm still living with her.
It's just rather than my wife my housekeeper.
At home, she always did everything - she cooked, washed, cleaned.
She's hardworking.
In my opinion, she has always tried so that I have no other reason to complain.
And you could say she's good.
But sometimes I'd like to pet her, hug her, and she won't.
We go for walks and shopping together and everyone thinks what a happy marriage we have.
She once told me that she never loved me.
That she liked and liked me, but that she didn't love me.
And I can see he loves him to this day, it's her god.
I don't enjoy such a life.
Even when it started and I carried it very badly, I once told her that I didn't want to live and I asked what she would say if it was over.
I wanted to know if she'd agree or not.
And that's what I wanted to hear from her: Yes or no.
But she thought and finally said, "But there's plenty of room."
Prime Minister Manuel Valls wants to establish a new French Islamic Foundation, which should serve as a bridge between the state and its Muslim population.
After the terrorist attacks, France escalates the fight against radical Islamists and calls for help from the government and the local Muslim minority.
"If Muslims themselves do not help the state against those who violate public freedom, it will become increasingly difficult for France to guarantee the freedom of their religion," Prime Minister Manuel Valls said.
He intends to ensure that only the clergy-educated in France are preached in the country.
He also wants to stop the funding of mosques from abroad, when jihad preachers often come up with money.
By October, Prime Minister Valls plans to establish a new French Islamic Foundation that should serve as a bridge between the state and its Muslim population.
The new institution will replace the current French Foundation for Islam, founded in 2005 by then-Prime Minister Dominique de Villepin.
Even then, it was supposed to contribute to the transparency of financial flows between mosques and the countries that finance their construction.
Soon, however, internal contradictions affected it, and it never worked properly.
"It's a total failure," Valls said.
At the head of the new foundation is likely to be former interior and defense minister Jean-Pierre Chevènement, who is known as a proponent of a secular state.
There are about 2500 mosques and prayer rooms in France, about 120 of which serve radical Islamism.
Since 2012, French authorities have expelled about 80 Islamic clerics from the country and are considering the move for dozens more.
This week, German police have also intervened against jihadist clerics.
Imams have reportedly recruited militants for the Islamic State.
One of them operated a space where worshippers allegedly radicalized and later attacked a Sikh temple in Essen.
Immediately after the Charlie Hebdo attacks in January 2015, there was a debate in France about a new institution to replace a dysfunctional foundation.
Other terrorist attacks carried out by radical Islamists in the name of the Islamic State have hastened everything.
The new foundation will have two main tasks − to ensure the financing of mosques from domestic sources and also oversee imams.
However, it is not yet clear where he will get his finances, even though several proposals are being considered.
One of them is a special fee for halal food, i.e. mainly for meat derived from defeats according to Islamic principles.
This tax would in practice be paid by sellers of halal food, namely for the provision of permits.
Some of the money would go to the foundation's budget.
A contribution of 10 to 20 euros could also be paid by French pilgrims going to Mecca.
Every year, about 30 thousand of them travel from France on a traditional pilgrimage to Saudi Arabia.
Moreover, only preachers who have undergone educational programs at local universities should work in French mosques.
As a result, their preaching should be consistent with democracy.
The government is also shutting down radical mosques.
"We have closed twenty of them since December, and others will follow," Interior Minister Bernard Cazeneuve said.
"There is no place in France for those who proclaim hatred and disrespect the principles of our country, such as equality between women and men," he said.
Zaragoza.
His ex-wife was badly injured by an unidentified shooter on Friday morning at a shopping mall in Zaragoza, northern Spain.
The shooting occurred after a woman and her daughter left the premises of the garage in their car.
The man waited in the car outside the shopping center, from where he also started shooting.
According to witnesses, the man then turned the gun against himself, the Independent writes.
The woman was taken to the hospital by an uninjured daughter, an ambulance had to arrive for a man in critical condition.
The act took place around 11.00 in the central part of the metropolis of the Autonomous Region of Aragonia, not far from the local university.
Citing Spain's Europa Press news agency, the Independent writes that the motive was probably an unresolved recent divorce of both partners.
Rolls with asparagus, wakame eyelashes, ginger and chilli sauce.
Summer wishes for light, well-digestible meals, but we Czechs are rebels and we have our tastes and our heads.
And so from the summer gardles smells of grilled necks, bellies, pork colognes.
Nothing very healthy, nothing very light, sometimes with the exception of lettuce or toasted vegetables.
True, the barbecue is energy-saving for time and dishes, in addition, it is a social matter and no one has to be confined in the kitchen.
