Britain must settle for silver in seven rugby, Fiji's selection takes gold
Great Britain's impressive performance in a seven-year rugby ended with a devastating lead at the last hurdle, with Fiji beating in the final match Thursday night by the British at Deodoro Stadium here in Rio high 43:7.
The Fijans performed a master's lesson in processing, landfilling, evasions, a supportive game, a run on the lane and brutal forces - and won gold, their first Olympic medal ever.
Defeat came with an unattracted consolation prize in the form of a silver medal - Britain's third on a given day - and the British could have been proud of their performances in the inaugural Olympic tournament of seven rugby, defeating New Zealand, Argentina and South Africa.
A hard-earned 7:5 win over South Africans in the semi-finals the same day is perhaps frosty in the final.
However, Fiji would also defeat the 15-member team in full health in its form.
It's not going to be a consolation for the British, but they can rejoice that at least one of them will have a gold medal around their necks tomorrow: the head coach of Fijans is Ben Ryan, England's former Wimbledon boss.
The UK has come a long way.
Eight years ago under Ryan, they lost every game in the Adelaide Sevens.
They lost only one here.
This defeat hurts, but progress is undeniable.
Fiji began spectacularly, reflecting the intersections of the British for joy.
For captain Tom Mitchell and other teammates, it was like running behind a number of buses on the move, and the World Cup champions led by 5:0 when Osa Kolinisau surfaced from around the corner.
The timer showed four minutes and Jerry Tuwai added another down on the right flank, from which was a conversion.
12:0 and Britain hardly touched the ball.
The great Mitchel tried to stop the huge Leon Nakarawa and prevent him from scoring for the third time, but only Fijans slowed down.
In the seventh minute, Jasa Veremalua threw the touchdown into the right corner and the British lost 17:0.
Nakarawa added more after his return, and there was still more than 1 minute left on the clock ... from the first half.
Vatemo Ravovou then penetrated the malaise British and rushed to the lane where he scored.
After convertinging to 29:0 was followed by a break.
It was as if Fiji was born for rugby 7s, while the British were still learning transmission.
That's the way it is.
After half-time, the British made mass changes and halted the onslaught of the Fijans... for a full four minutes, which was good compared to the first half, as long as he didn't pass further after Fiji Josu Tuisov's brilliant interplay.
Dan Norton tried to get an opportunity for the British, throwing a touchdown into the left corner to increase to 36:7. However, the match had long been won by residents of the Pacific Islands, and their fans sang, waving flags and dancing.
It was hard not to admire the utterly joyful attitude of the Fijans.
In the final moments, Mata Viliame sweetened with another attempt to win the Fijans.
There was a whistle, the Fijans embraced, and the British fell onto the lawn, completely overwhelmed.
Even the same day, Fiji beat Japan 20:5 in the semi-finals, and Great Britain beat South Africa in a tight semi-final - the difference of one conversion.
Kyle Brown got the South Africans into the lead in the first half, but Dan Norton, thanks to his dazzling footworks, broke through the defensive line and scored in the second half under the hook.
Marcus Watson's great late landfill, brother Anthony of England's 15th-ranked team, ensured victory - and, ultimately, a silver medal.
Helen Glover and Heather Stanning were inspired to win the rowing race by coach-cancer
You won't find more emotive gold in these Games - and that's something to say.
From the first shot to the last, during 220 punishing exhalations into history, Helen Glover and Heather Stanning found further significance in their golden moment at Estacio de Lagoa.
Even though there were two sitting at the oar, there were three votes in the boat. When the boat whizzed down the surface towards the second Olympic gold in a row in the women's doubles race, there was still the voice of the still-president coach Robin Williams. They are the first women in the history of British sport to achieve such a result.
Their superiority was absolute, overall it was their 39th race, in which they remained unbeaten.
What we're seeing - two excellent athletes flying on the water surface with the elegance of a swan - doesn't reflect the nature of victory altogether.
For every gold medal of rowing, there's a battalion of auxiliary hands, which, bit by bit, makes a difference. And no hands were more helpful than the hands of a coach who didn't know 30 months ago whether he's going to live this day when he's been diagnosed with cancer.
According to him, it was uncertain.
I suffered from bladder cancer, which can be operated on, and I underwent this surgery.
Without an operation, anything could happen.
It would be a very bad situation.
It'll change your point of view somewhat.
The cancer was detected by Williams in December 2013.
Luckiness for the entire trio was that he was able to receive treatment in early 2014, out of season, to shorten the blackout to a minimum - if we can call it that in such circumstances.
I needed to make money, so I had to get together quickly.
That was a stimulus.
I was in the hospital and I was walking around the room - 20 feet, 30 feet, 50 feet at a time they said a few steps were enough.
That's how it started.
Rowing in itself is a journey, but this was a bit more challenging.
Immediately after the win, their first thoughts about Williams, who was very nervous in the morning, spoke less than usual as he tried to handle his own uneasiness.
Stanning, captain of the Royal Artillery Regiment, who has a mission in Afghanistan behind her, said: "I've been with emotions down this week, maybe it's a little extreme, but it means so much."
It highlights once again how much this means to me, how much Helen and I worked, and so did Robin.
Until now, it's been three fantastic years with Helen and Robin.
We'd be nobody without Robin, so he's a huge thank, he's the best coach in the world.
I couldn't have asked for a better person for training or a better coach.
Helen Glover and Heather Stanning have not lost a single race in five years.
The pair put on a staggering rhythm that their rivals couldn't cope with.
Even in the final 500 meters, when crews from New Zealand and Denmark began to approach, Stanning and Glover had a very large reserve.
At the very least, we were focused at the moment.
Helen shouted, “Don’t stop, don’t stop.”
It's really important not to get carried away.
We had a good start and at the same time we didn't want to achieve something fabulous.
We weren't looking forward to today's fireworks, we just wanted to row well, go out and win the race, which we did.
The pair take a year to think before deciding what's next.
Glover is getting married next week, with TV adventurer Steva Backshall.
Now he wants to enjoy the moment.
I didn't want to say this was going to be my last race
That would put too much pressure.
It took about six months in London to find out I'm an Olympic champion.
It was all so new and I was so stressed.
It took about four minutes here.
I have a much better feeling about it," she said.
Seat in the first row to the magnificent architecture of the Central Library in Los Angeles
The Society of Architectural Historians/Southern California Chapter organizes meetings with Arnold Schwartzman and Stephen Gee, the authors of the new book “Central Library in Los Angeles: The History of Its Art and Architecture.”
Gee and Schwartzman will speak and show images of Bertram Goodhue's 1926 design and the restoration and completion of 1933 by Hardy Holzman Pfeiffer Associates.
The meeting will take place from 14 to 16 o'clock on 21 August at Salle Moderne at the Southwestern School of Law Art Deco (formerly Bullocks Wilshire), at 3050 Wilshire Blvd.
Participants can view the Central Hall, Period Rooms, Cactus Lounge lounge and tea room.
It is necessary to buy a ticket in advance: SAH/SCC members 15 USD, non-members 25 USD.
An open industrial loft in DTLA will undergo a cosy reconstruction.
