Who is benefiting from rising tensions around Crimea?
The Ukrainian army.
In recent days and hours, the situation on the Ukrainian-Russian border in the Crimea region has deteriorated.
Who benefits from rising tensions?
After a long period of relative calm, relations between Ukraine and Russia deteriorated again.
The pretext of the Ukrainian side became the peninsula of Crimea, which was annexed more than 2 years ago to Russia and is part of its territory.
Of course, the news from both opposing parties contradicts each other and it is difficult to independently verify them, and so do we have the opportunity only to reflect on the old well-known question of who benefits from it?
According to reports from Moscow, there have been terrorist attacks on Crimea from the Ukrainian side, which, of course, Ukraine refuses and, on the contrary, accuses the Kremlin of provocation.
A well-known response to the information about the arrest of members of Ukrainian special forces on the territory of Russia came from the case of the Savchenko pilot that they were allegedly kidnapped on the territory of Russia.
If we agreed to the Ukrainian version, in what way would Russia benefit from the deterioration of the situation around Crimea?
The simple answer is: nothing.
The escalation of tensions around Crimea can only harm Russia, mainly on the part of the West in the form of a further hardening of sanctions and intensification of the anti-Russian campaign.
In the last year there has been a retreat from the situation in Ukraine against the backdrop of the immigration crisis in Europe.
The Ukrainian leadership has already completely lost credit in front of its inhabitants, when the current support of President Poroshenko is around 15%.
Ukraine's economy would have gone bankrupt long ago without generous financial injections by the EU and especially the IMF.
In this situation, the Ukrainian regime needs once again vigorously drawing attention to its country in order to justify its further requests for financial assistance and to try to improve its reputation before voters in their country.
President Poroshenko and other members of the leadership of the forces again want to turn out to be strong men who are not afraid to stand up to the big Russia.
We can only hope that all those who are trying to unleash an open conflict between Ukraine and Russia will eventually realize their enormous responsibility for the lives not only of soldiers, but of all ordinary people on both opposing sides and stop their hostilities in time.
For the first time in eight months, Bulgaria reports inflation
According to national statistics, the monthly inflation rate in Bulgaria reached 1% in July.
This is the highest figure in several years (since June 2012) and the first non-zero value since October last year, when a positive inflation rate of 0.2% was published.
The annual average inflation rate, measured by the CPI, over the last 12 months (August 2015 - July 2016) compared to the previous 12-month period (August 2014 - July 2015) was -0.8%.
The consumer price index in July 2016 compared to June 2016 was 101.0%, so monthly inflation was 1.0%.
The inflation rate since the beginning of the year (July 2016 compared to December 2015) was -0.2%, and the annual inflation rate in July 2016 compared to July 2015 was -0.2%.
Compared to the previous month, there was an increase in trips of the following groups of consumer goods in July 2016: food and soft drinks (1.7%), transportation (1.8%), culture and recreation (6.3%), restaurants and hotels (1.2%), other goods and services (0.3%).
There was a decrease in prices for clothing and footwear (2.2%), furniture, household equipment and routine maintenance (0.5%), health (0.1%).
At the same level as the previous month, prices remained for alcoholic beverages and tobacco products, housing, water, electricity, gas and other fuels, communications and education.
