To prison for money.
In Scotland you can go to prison even without committing a crime.
For many centuries, Inveraray was the county centre and seat of the Duke of Argyll.
From the middle of the 18th century, courts were held at the town hall, and the basement under the hall served as a local prison.
The conditions in which the convicts dwelt were pitiful and many prisoners tried to escape.
Many of them even successfully.
That's what the city's representatives decided to move the prison premises to a new location.
The new building was completed in 1848 and was much more friendly to prisoners, had heating and gas lighting.
Either way it has not been in operation for a long time, for economic reasons it was closed at the end of August 1889, because smaller correctional facilities were expensive to operate and did not pay much.
Stories of convicts and the hunt for ghosts.
The prison opened to the public a century later, visitors can compare the old building with a new one in order to see for themselves how different the conditions prevailed.
Guides in period costumes enhance the authentic experience.
There is also a torture room in which visitors can learn what forms of punishment were applied to criminals, whether it was breaking their fingers or burning marks on the body.
As part of the tour, tourists can be confined to one of the cells, mostly because of a photo in memory, or to evoke the feeling experienced by prisoners behind bars.
Moreover, Inveraray prison is one of Scotland's best-known locations where it supposedly scares.
There have already been several groups of paranormal hunters, and to this day the museum allows people to come to hunt some ghosts.
Such a night, however, comes to 300 pounds, which is about 9500 crowns.
In addition to this popular tourist attraction, municipalities are also attracted to great food, surrounding panoramas and history.
"Animé" effect on Snapchat is criticized as an Asian cartoon
According to Snapchat, this filter is inspired by anime, but some observers say it is racially insensitive.
The Snapchat app allows about 150 million users every day to change reality and play with the identity of ways bordering on absurdity.
You can make a pineapple of yourself, a dog or a character who seems to have stepped out of Roy Lichtenstein's painting.
Filters are simple tools that distort reality and generate more than 30 million improved selfies every day.
Any error will be published immediately.
Snapchat's filters have earned criticism in the past with allegations that the app promotes blackfacing or that it promotes white shades of skin as an ideal of beauty.
So, when this week it offered users a filter that made their eyes sloped, their teeth twisted, and bloated cheeks, some critics called it a racist cartoon of Asians - "yellowface."
And they asked if these repeated contentious issues did not point to society's bigger problem - the diversity problem.
On Wednesday, Verge and Motherboard wrote about it, and the novelty and indignation spread quickly, and Snapchat said the very next day that it had canceled the feature.
The company offered an explanation: The filter was intended as a manifestation of the accounts to the characters animé, not as a caricature of Asians.
But to observers who have witnessed racism, the filter has reminded painful stereotypes in action.
Others, unpacked compared to the animé, declined.
Grace Sparapani, a student of American art from Korea, whose tweet about the photos was widely shared, said in an email that the filter was "at least harmful and embarrassing."
She added that "it's hard to argue against a comparison of an obvious Asian cartoon and filter effects.
It turns out that the filter is not just about the yellow face (yellowface), but that the yellow face is drawn to a contemptuous extreme.
Snapchat is not the only society that has exceeded these cultural limits.
It seems that American culture has an endless struggle for diversity and inclusion, from boardrooms in corporations to Holywood and devices that we all carry in our hands.
And a huge number of young people on Snapchat - who are more racially diversified than their older partners - can mean that even greater sensitivity can be expected.
“When one Snapchat filter creates an image that’s offensive to some users,” twenty-five-year-old Katie Zhu said in an interview on Thursday, “it’s very difficult to do these things unnoticed today, like they used to.”
Katie Zhu, a product manager and designer who works for Medium on Thursday, decided to delete her Snapchat account and urged others to do the same.
In an article for Medium and in an interview over the phone, she said she was convinced that racially motivated issues reflected the lack of diversity in recruiting Snapchat workers.
Katie Zhu criticised the overwhelming whites, completely male management of the company, and the article ended with a hash tag: #DeleteSnapchat.
“It’s either that they don’t have any diversity among the employees at the moment they make decisions about these things,” said Zhu, who has a Chinese-American background, “or colored people work there, but not in positions to make them feel safe or comfortable to talk.”
Other observers share her opinion - a complaint Snapchat left largely unanswered.
The company does not publish figures relating to the diversity of its employees, saying it has the status of a private company.
On Thursday Snapchat declined to speak about the racial origins of its employees, but, according to a spokesman, the company recently recruited, which should focus on an underrepresented population and internal management of inclusion.
“I’m curious if they really need other users who can say it’s not okay,” she said.
Army officers misrepresent intelligence on IS, parliamentary committee says
In a report issued on Thursday, a parliamentary committee said officers from the U.S. Central Headquarters had changed intelligence reports to portray a more optimistic picture of the war against the Islamic State in Iraq and in Syria than provided by events from the country.
According to a preliminary report by a working group compiled by the Republican chairman of the Armed Forces Committee, the Intelligence Committee and the Defense Subcommittee, "extensive dissatisfaction" was found among central intelligence analysts, according to which their superiors changed their assessment of U.S. efforts to defeat the Islamic State.
The Central Headquarters, known as CentCom, is the Army's central in Tampa, Florida, which oversees U.S. military operations in the Middle East and Central Asia.
“The outputs of the intelligence secret services approved by Centcom’s superior officers usually offered a more positive picture of US counter-terrorism efforts than those confirmed by facts on earth, and were consistently more positive than the analyses obtained from other parts of the intelligence community.”
"What's happened at Centcom is unacceptable - our fighters are harmed when a misanagement is presented to superior policy makers," said Ken Calvert, a congressman and Republican from California.
The failure to lead Centcom goes all the way to the top of the organization.
The 10-page report describes in detail the persistent problems of the central staff in the description and analysis of U.S. efforts in training Iraqi forces in 2014 and 2015.
While there is no decisive evidence available that senior officials of the Obama administration ordered the reports to be altered, the report said the analysts had the impression that they were under pressure from Centcom's leaders to present a more optimistic view of the danger posed by the Islamic State, or IS.
"In the first half of 2015, many press releases of the central staff, statements and statements from the congress were substantially more positive than what the events actually were," the report said.
For example, a Centcom official has publicly stated that a major military attack to take over Mosul could begin as early as April or May 2015.
Mosul, Iraq's second-largest city, remains under Islamic State control.
"This is quite clear after months of investigation," MP Mike Pompeo, a representative of the Kansas Republican Party, said in a statement.
From mid-2014 to mid-2015, the highest-ranking U.S. Central Staff intelligence personnel manipulated the outputs of staff intelligence in order to downplay the dangers of IS in Iraq.
Republicans have assembled a working group after learning about analysts' concerns that intelligence information related to the Islamic State is being manipulated.
The report, published on Thursday, must be followed by a more detailed investigation after further investigation.
There has been yet another investigation into Centcom's intelligence services, led by the Defense Department's inspector general.
Democratic members of the Intelligence Committee released their own findings on Thursday, which matched some of the conclusions of the Republican working group.
"Between 2014 and 2015, Centcom has created a very narrow process for compiling evaluations of information about IS and Iraqi security forces," Representative Adam B. Schiff, Chairman of the Committee and Democrat Representative, said in a statement.
According to Adam Schiff, this process "led to a distortion of intelligence outlets," damaging morale among analysts and "doing not adequately take into account dissenting views."
However, Schiff and Democrats say they have found no evidence that the White House was trying to pressure Centcom analysts to adjust their conclusions according to a "model or political situation."
Preliminary results of the parliamentary investigation were published in the Daily Beast on Tuesday.
Last August, the New York Times reported an ongoing investigation by the Pentagon's inspector-general, which was launched after complaints from Centcom analysts.
Lieutenant Commander Patrick Evans, a Pentagon spokesman, said in a statement that the Department of Defense would not comment on the parliamentary report because the inspector-general's investigation is still ongoing.
“Experts sometimes disagree on the interpretation of complex data, and that the intelligence community and the Ministry of Defence welcome a sound dialogue about these fundamental issues of national security.”
The investigation has implications beyond the question of whether American-led bombing in Iraq and Syria is successful (a statement that now declares a growing number of defense leaders).
However, last year's allegations prompted questions about the extent to which President Obama can count on the central staff sincerely evaluating military operations in Iraq, Afghanistan, Lybia, and other emergencies.
Criticism of intelligence about the Islamic State reflects conflicts for more than a decade, when Centcom intelligence analyst Gregory Hooker wrote a research paper statining that President George W. Bush's administration - despite the objections of many analysts - defended a small unit in Iraq and devoted little time to thinking about what would follow the 2003 invasion.
Hooker has also played a key role in opposition to the Islamic State's intelligence services.
Last year, authorities announced that Hooker's team had concluded that, despite public statements to the contrary, aerial strikes conducted against Islamic State refineries did not significantly weaken the terrorist group's financial resources because the group had built temporary refineries and was selling oil on the black market.
The Times reported in September that the findings had not been handed over outside the central staff.
Centcom's commander, Gen. Lloyd J. Austin III, was then subjected to criticism in the past year after many lawmakers found testimony to the Senate's over-positive assessment of the war.
General Austin resigned in April and was replaced in Centcom by General Joseph L. Votel.
The U.S
Katie Bam scored three wickets in the match against Japan, which ended with a 6:1 victory, and just a day after that for the U.S. women's field hockey team scored the first two goals of the match in which America beat India 3:0 on Thursday.
Five wickets in two games is a success worth remembering, but Bam (27) said she wasn't sure she'd ever managed to do anything like that.
"I don't know," she said.
I'm not one of those people who records their wickets.
But that's what he knows: the Americans in the Olympic tournament are leading for 4:0 matches and sharing first place in a group with Great Britain remaining unbeaten on Thursday after Japan's 2:0 defeat.
Great Britain and the United States will clash on Saturday in the final match of the first round.
