The team from the first place will avoid a little longer the strong Danes, who are at the top of the second group.
No matter how Saturday's game ends, the Americans caused a stir here with their surprise ride, four years after they finished among the 12 teams at the London Games.
Bam was on that team, too.
She grew up in Blue Bell, in Pennsylvania, and picked up a field hockey stick for the first time when she was 3 years old (her older twin sisters played in Drexel) and joined the national team when she was 16 and became his youngest player ever.
She escaped the 2008 Olympics because she didn't make it to the final roster.
Then came 2012, when neither she nor the members of her team were doing very well.
The next year, Englishman Craig Parnham was named coach, and things began to change.
"Cultural change, mental change, physical change," Bam said.
We didn't have the pace of work in London like we have today.
We've made a complete turnaround.
On Thursday night, Bam started in the first half by scoring the goal with a bekend from close range.
India entered the match without winning the tournament, but the team proved surprisingly tough and kept the score 1:0 until the end of the third quarter when Bam took the opportunity.
She escaped on the left side of the playing field, then zigzagged into the middle and scored the ball into the goal to get the Americans into a 2:0 lead.
Melissa Gonzalez added her final wicket in the middle of the fourth quarter.
Eliminating the American goalie, Jackie Briggs, who in each of the three victories helped to one goal, was the first in the tournament.
He's in excellent shape.
Just like Bama.
"We're in a good place now," Parnham said, but how good it is, it will depend on the outcome of Saturday's game.
Zen trampolines in the middle of an Olympic rage
It doesn't matter where you are, it doesn't matter what time it's - it's the Olympics.
From the events in Rio de Janeiro you can feel persistent, indescribable and biased alternation of competition and effort.
It's how the Olympic torch itself would be a hot relay peg being handed over from a chandelier by a swimmer, a gymnast and a triathlete.
You can't turn on TV, read a newspaper or listen to your neighbor's conversation in a queue at a coffee shop, without hearing about Simone Biles, Katie Ledecky or the animosity between Michael Phelps and Chad Le Close.
And then there's Logan Dooley.
Dooley, the upcoming sweetheart of women with dimples in his faces, is at the Olympics for the first time and could also be among those whose names will be heard in households in prime-time between commercials and spot sponsors if he were a swimmer, gymnast or even a swordsman.
But Dooley (28) of Lake Forest, California, is battling for Olympic gold in trampoline jumps.
The Olympic Flame doesn't shine the same on all competitions.
Away from the center, a little in the shade are sports that are not universally recognized: air pistol shooting, archery, badminton, taekvondo.
We'll always have success in the classic swimming style - and it'll probably rule for a long time.
But if I can name my extraordinary favorite, let me draw attention to the inconspicuous magic of the trampoline.
Although jumping on trampolines has been an Olympic sport since 2000, when they were first introduced at the Sydney Games, after 16 years of intervention, the sport remains almost unnoticed - many of the people I inquired didn't even know it was an Olympic sport.
It could be regional indifference.
The U.S. has never won a medal in trampoline jumps, and none of the athletes made it to the finals until 2012.
Prices in the men's category are usually divided between China and Russia, and in the women's category between China and Canada.
This year's gold medals from London will be embraced by Dong Dong from China and Rosie MacLennan of Canada.
But there's even more unluck on the trampoline, partly because the uninitiated's ear will seem more suited to a clown university than the Olympics.
I have to confess that I am not an expert, but only an enthusiast watching from the country.
It has comical potential - the comical vibrations of endless repulsion - but also the gracefulness of gymnastics and diving, the two sports it combines.
The word “trampoline” is derived from the Spanish “el trampoline”, which means “jumping slab.”
Aspiring champions jump on a huge trampoline that shoots them to a height of up to almost 10 meters and showcases line-ups with several elements (salts, screws and combinations) before they finally land on their feet again.
According to the official Olympic program, they are evaluated according to execution, difficulty and "time in the air."
Thirty-three athletes from 17 countries, 16 men and 16 women will compete in Rio.
The usual trajectory of the Olympic track is forward.
Fighters for gold medals have to go further, faster and even higher, sometimes to the end, other times back and forth, up and down.
It tries to catch up not only with the finish tape or the starting wall, but, as the TV news reports, also another imaginary line: a world or Olympic record.
This unanimoned effort is noble, admirable, a little fanatical - and sufficient to bring the audience to tears.
But I think it's also somewhat exhausting when you're watching the Olympics.
Jumping on a trampoline in this regard brings relief.
The track of an athlete on a trampoline is upwards.
The point is not to move forward.
He or it is reflected, as if they are unheaviled like astronauts, flying up, then down, and then again effortlessly up.
Without competition and, of course, it is not possible, but for the duration of the line-up, there is no furious fight to achieve a goal, no distance measurement, but only a perfect, graceful flexibility of time and gravity.
This is just the closest that the Olympics and zen will come closer.
Qualifications and women's finals take place on Friday afternoons, at 13 o'clock eastern times, men's competitions then on Saturday at the same time.
NBC will broadcast the finals as part of an afternoon block that begins for both days at 2 p.m.
In the middle of the Olympic frenzy, they promise calm, pleasant moments, which you will probably miss despite the other fights.
And if attention is to be achieved only in such a way that male athletes will be the subject of in-loving glimpses and materialization - as according to the Wall Street Journal recently designed by U.S. gymnasts - Logan Dooley is merely waiting for his new fans gasping.
“Protesting generation” in Ethiopia, a long-standing U.S
Violent protests in Ethiopia
Protesters demanding political changes in Ethiopia have encountered violent opposition from the government.
Witnesses say many protesters have been shot dead during clashes with police.
Is there a threat of war in Ethiopia?
Over the past decade, it has been one of Africa's most stable nations, a solid Western alliance with a fast-growing economy.
In recent months, however, anti-government unrest has engulfed the country spreading to more and more areas.
In the last week alone, thousands of people have taken to the streets demanding major political changes.
According to human-rights organizations, the government's response was ruthless.
Witnesses say officers shot and killed crowds of unarmed protesters.
On circulating videotapes from the protests, which probably came from the end of last year or earlier this year, members of the security forces are captured beating young people with sticks after being forced to stand by the wall.
The U.N. High Commissioner for Human Rights is now requesting a thorough investigation.
“It has always been difficult to keep this country together and taking a step forward will be even harder,” said Rashid Abdi, Horn of Africa project leader for the International Crisis Group.
Ethiopia is Africa's second most populous nation after Nigeria, and its stability is supported by the West.
U.S. military and intelligence agencies are working closely with Ethiopians to eliminate the dangers of a terrorist attack in the area, especially in Somalia, and few of the African countries, if any, are gaining such assistance from the West.
Ethiopia's economy has evolved at an impressive pace.
Its infrastructure has fundamentally improved - there is even a new suburban train in the capital Addis Abeba.
And the streets of the city are typically quiet, safe and clean.
However, Ethiopia can hardly be described as a model of democracy - human-rights organizations constantly speak of government repression - the opposition in the country has been suppressed, dissidents effectively silenced.
Many have been banished, imprisoned, killed, or driven far into the desert.
However, this may change.
“When you’re suffocating people and they have no choice but to protest, there’s going to be a breach,” said Seyoum Teshome, who lectures at the University of Central Ethiopia.
Protesting all the youth.
A whole generation is protesting.
Complaints are many and relate to everything from land disputes to the absolute control of the power of the ruling coalition.
After last year's elections, which were widely criticized, the ruling party and its allies won the last seat held by the opposition and now controls 100 percent in parliament.
At the same time, there is growing pressure on the border with Eritrea. The conflict along this inaccurately marked, contentious boundary claimed hundreds of lives in June.
Anyliticians fear that separatist groups that have been more or less suppressed over the past years, such as the Oroma Liberation Front or the Ogaden National Liberation Front, can try to exploit the confusion and re-arm themselves.
There are several factors that explain why now, after years of smoldering beneath the surface, these bitter sensations flared up again.
The first is seemingly harmless: smartphones.
Many Ethiopians can communicate through social networks for up to the last few years, when cheaper smartphones have become commonly available and the provision of internet services has improved.
And even if the government disables access to Facebook or Twitter, as is commonly done primarily during protests, many people can still communicate via internet proxies that hide the location of these people.
Several young Ethiopians have spoken out about how they were rallying for protests.
There is also greater solidarity between the Oroms and Amharas, the two largest ethnic groups in Ethiopia.
Oroms and Amhars aren't natural allies.
For the long ages, the Amhars, mainly from the Christian hills of Ethiopia, excelled in politics and commerce and used the Oromas, many of whom are Muslims and live in the lowlands.
But that's changing, too.
"We are on the path to unified leadership," said Mulatu Gemech, the leader of the Oroms.
The biggest protests occurred in areas inhabited by Amhara and Oroma.
Many Amhars and Oromas believe that Ethiopia is wrongly dominated by members of the Tigray ethnic group, which accounts for about 6 percent of the population and controls the military, intelligence services, business and politics.
The third reason for the unrest is the death of Meles Zenawi.
Zenawi, the former leader of the insurgents, was an Ethiopian prime minister for 17 years until his death in 2012, when he succumbed to an undisclosed illness.
He was considered a tactical genius, a man who could see around the corner.
Analysts say he was particularly masterfully able to reveal initial signals of discontent and use envoys to process the opponents and get them to his side.
"The current regime lacks basic knowledge," said Abdi, an analyst at the conflict.
Ethiopia's new prime minister, Hailemariam Desalegn, has been pulled out of relative unambiguity in order to replace Zenawi.
Unlike Zenawi, who came from the Tigrayan region of northern Ethiopia, Hailemariam comes from the south.
Analysts say he has no confidence in security services controlled by Tigreans.
Many are concerned that the result will be a further shedding of blood.
The last time Ethiopia experienced such riots was in 2005 after thousands of people protested against what analysts described as an election that the government had seized and then stolen.
This was followed by a harsh crackdown during which many protesters were killed - albeit less than in the past months - and this period of rioting passed relatively quickly.
Development professionals have praised Ethiopia's leaders for visionary infrastructure planning, such as the new suburban train and measurable advances in fighting poverty.
However, it is quite obvious that the gradual increase in disputes within the Ethiopian government has not stopped it.
And there's a dangerous ethnic formation.
Last month, protesters in Gondar's Amhar city attacked trading firms owned by Tigreans and resentment against Tigreans on social media became increasingly common.
Analysts say the protest puts the U.S. and other Western allies in an uncomfortable situation.
The U.S. government is using Ethiopia as a base for overflights of drones over neighboring Somalia, although it has recently declared that the base has been closed.
While the West clearly wants to support democracy, at the same time it does not want its ally to disintegrate in an already volatile area.
"That," Abdi said, "it's a very sharp edge."
Donald Trump explains his remarks about Obama and IS funding as “sarcasm”
Donald J. Trump on Friday tried to downplay the latest controversy flooding his campaign by saying he wasn't serious when he insisted several times this week that Presiden Obama and Hillary Clinton were "founders" of the Islamic State terrorist group.
The explanation raised new questions about the Republican presidential candidate's ability to speak clearly with the U.S. public.
He also puts his proponents in an uncomfortable situation, who defended him hard in the tumultuous time and have to explain that he may not have been serious about his remarks.
After that motion at a meeting on Wednesday night, Trump upheld the statement on Thursday, insisting in interviews that he did not really intend to say that President and H. Clinton had created IS.
However, in a Twitter post on Friday morning, Trump declared that he was merely sarcastic.
The post was the opposite of a claim made by an old just one day when Trump, in several interviews, said he was serious about his allegations.
When Conservative radio presenter Hugh Hewitt tried to help Trump's remarks eased by reminding that Obama was seeking to destroy IS, the Republican nominee did not respond to the presidency.
The latest storm comes as Trump has attempted to focus his campaign's attention on the economy, while his preferences in pre-election polls are declining.
Trump and Clinton took part in a duel on economic issues in Michigan this week.
On Friday, Trump attempted to return to the subject again.
On Twitter, he once again warned that Clinton's proposals were linked to tax hikes, and stated that, according to him, her speech was boring.
Trump has used sarcasm defenses before.
At the end of July, when he reaped a wave of criticism when he came forward with a suggestion that the Russians should penetrate Clinton's e-mails, he later explained his remark by saying, "I'm sarcastic, of course."
This approach allows Trump to push the boundaries of political statements and moderate them as necessary.
“I think it’s his way of testing the extent to which some of the reports have a response from his supporters to find out how they react, and according to the reaction then, in one or the other way, he ‘given an explanation’,” said Ruth Sherman, a communications specialist who followed the language that both candidates used.
But I also think he's skillful in his statements and he knows they're vague enough to explain them differently.
As the results of pre-election polls in his favor continue to decline, Trump has chosen an even more sly tone in recent days, instead of moderating things for general voters.
The new NBC/Wall Street Journal/Marist survey, released on Friday, has shown that Tump is with great detachment behind Clinton in North Carolina, Virginia and Colorado, and very closely behind her in Florida.
Trump is even attempting to avoid apologies for provocative statements he says, but often accuses his critics of misinterpreting his words or misunderstanding his sense of humor.
But along with how Trump faces increased attention in the final months of the presidential campaign, Trump's jokes put in an uncomfortable situation his devoted supporters, who must publicly stand behind him, however provocative his remarks may be.
Rudolph W. Giuliani, former mayor of New York City, who is one of Trump's most prominent supporters, had to defend the candidate extensively in an interview with CNN on Thursday.
"I think what he's saying is a legitimate political commentary," Giuliani said when asked about the credibility of Trump's claims that Obama and Clinton founded the Islamic State.
That's true in the sense that before Obama, ISIS was almost unknown, a small organization that he erroneously called a rookie, and this happened because he withdrew his troops from Iraq.
Most ordinary Republicans are attempting to argue that the Obama administration, in which Clinton served as secretary of state, has allowed IS to flourish and not be able to handle the Middle East situation aggressively.
Before Friday's rallies in Pennsylvania, Trump continued to rain the fire to the media on Twitter for pondering his every word and biased against him.
He laughed at the “poor, pathetic” television scholars who were trying to understand it and said, “They can’t!”
Several Democrats grimbled Trump's apology, saying it's just further proof that he's not eligible for the presidency.
The cafe has pushed prices out of the market. It's now coming back in Greenpoint
Verb Cafe was a shabby place in Williamsburg, where you could only pay in cash, without Wi-Fi, but where coffee has been served for 15 years.
Naturally, it occurred that she had been pushed out from the faces of a house on Bedford Avenue.
Soap Cherie - a store Verb replaced in the summer of 2014 - sells soaps in the shape of cupcakes.
The locals mourned it as the last loss of a growing city.
Artist Molly Crabapple tweeted: "New York is a change, I know, but I still can't believe CBGB is dead, a Chelsea hotel plundered, and Verb is now a shop soap."
But if New York is a change, then it's also rediscovery.
The resurrected Verb Cafe opened last November in Greenpoint, less than a mile north of its original location.
The new Verb has wifi and accepts credit cards, but some of the original employees work there and there is a similar, modest atmosphere.
The coffee is still good too.
The manager of the new Verbu is Cisco Rodriguez, a long-time employee of the original cafe, who is also the owner and executive director of the new site.
It started in Verb in 2001 and remained until the end.
When one of the customers offered to pay for the move to a new location, he said he would do it with pleasure.
Rodriguez (36) left a lot of things as they were.
Verb still has the same pastry supplier and bought the same espresso machine model.
He even reused the lighting from old Verbu.
Rodriguez, however, said he also wanted to try something new.
He started using new ingredients.
There are ham sandwiches, eggs and cheese and toppings such as capers and fried onions.
You'll also find bowls of brown rice.
The café no longer serves beer, but offers you fresh fruit juice.
After nine months, Rodriguez noticed a steady tide of original regulars.
“A number of original customers also had to evict themselves from Williamsburg because of prices,” he said.
Last Sunday there was one of such customers, Rebecca Oliveira.
Ms. Oliveira (34) lived in Williamsburg and went to Verb Cafe often.
She even went then when the cafe moved to Greenpoint.
She said when Verb closed, she completely stopped drinking coffee for some time.
"I've found that what I like is coffee and culture in Verbu," she said.
Now she's drinking coffee again, but on that day, instead of her usual iced coffee, she had mixed chai, another novelty.
Tom Rosenthal, another long-time customer, has been going to Verbu since 2003.
He goes before work and always orders the same thing.
“At quarter and eight I come here every day like a clock, and they are already preparing for me my bun with everything and butter,” he said.
Photo: Rodriguez said he wanted to try something new and add new ingredients to the menu.
Mr. Rosenthal continues to order what he usually does, but he occasionally puts bacon, eggs and ham on his bun with everything.
Rodriguez said he wasn't sorried that the soap shop had taken his old place.
The truth is that Soap Cherie's owners have offered to make a special soap for Verb and use filtered coffee from the cafe as an ingredient.
"I think we could still take advantage of that," Rodriguez said.
Also the traditional Polish residents of Greenpoint accepted the cafe well.
One of the new customers translated the offer into Polish and Rodriguez said he wanted to find a place to put it soon, so that his new neighbors would feel at home here.
The new place has other advantages.
A local fruit juice maker wants to bottle and sell filtered coffee.
There is an opportunity for the café to spread to a neighboring building.
It is enough for many customers to get back their old favorite.
Joseph Whitt was a regular customer, and when the cafe closed, it broke his heart.
Mr. Whitt says that when the cafe reopened, to enter and have a coffee is like coming home.
"I missed the smell," he said.
Depressive food from the time of the crisis - in "Square Meal"
Shortly after taking office as president in March 1933, Franklin Delano Roosevelt sat in the Oval Office and had lunch.
Gourmet President Roosevelt enjoyed selected dishes from the 5th avenue, such as goose yummy pâté and Maryland turtle soup.
However, his menu for this day was more modest: eggs on the devilish way in tomato sauce, potato puree and, as a dessert, plum pudding.
"It was an act of cooking solidarity with the people who suffered," Jane Ziegelman said.
Her husband Andrew Coe added: "It was also a message to Americans on how to eat."
The couple, who lives in Brooklyn Heights, is dedicated to gastronomic history.
Andrew Coe's latest book "Chop Suey" was about Chinese cuisine in America, while Jane, in her book "97 Orchard," tells the life story of a residential home on the Lower East Side through food.
Their new "A Square Meal," published by Harper on Tuesday, is the history of U.S. cuisine during the Great economic crisis.
They themselves showed cooking solidarity and met with a reporter for dinner at Eisenberg's sandwich shop, a small austere buffet in Flatiron, which has been in operation since 1929.
Jane (54) ordered cream cheese and sandwich with chopped olives, Andrew (57) had turkey, mashed potatoes and scrambled vegetables.
The reporter ordered a meat meatball, which, according to the couple, was perfectly suited to talking about food during the crisis.
“Meatballs were very popular,” said Miss Ziegelman.
It sold peanut, liver and bean meatball.
They were made from that admixture and other cheap food that set the ingredient.
Think about the amount of peanuts you’d have to eat for dinner.
Andrew Coe grinned.
"They'd lie in your stomach like lead," he said.
In the years before the crisis, American cuisine, especially in rural areas, was served in the form of a buffet of what you eat.
Women's teams cooked for farm workers, and for breakfast, lunch, and dinner, a freshly baked cake was served.
It was the Americans who sent food to starving Europeans during the First World War. “A Square Meal” observes the ways in which the nation coped with the fact that suddenly it was not a country of abundance.
"This was a time when food became the focal, dreaded point for the American people," Coe said, explaining why he and his wife decided to write about it.
Equally dreaded was the new budget dietary: mysterious meals spread, such as meatballs and casserole meals, casses as "an amazing way to take advantage of leftovers," Ziegelman commented, as chefs could hide unaesthetic ingredients under a layer of creamy sauce.
Fresh fruit has been replaced by cheaper dried fruits.
Meat - the focal point of U.S
Strange mixtures, which were supposed to serve as a caloric and nutritional filling, took precedence over taste or even common sense when cooked.
During surveys for a book that contains recipes, Ms. Ziegelman prepared a period dish of baked onions stuffed with peanut butter.
"It wasn't a favorite addition to the evening table," Andrew Coe said.
Jane Ziegelman emphasized: "It was surreal."
Peanut butter with roasted onions doesn't go together.
It was typical of a lot of homemade recipes.
As never before and never after, domestic economists - among them Louise Stanley, the head of the federal office for the domestic economy between 1923 and 1943 - managed the country's eating habits.
They published recipes and articles in newspapers and magazines, urged women to take the reins of the budget, and they converted scum spaghetti with carrots into tasty meals.
“A Square Meal” is a celebration of historical delicacies.
Above all, desserts were created in the government’s response to its hungry citizens, such as President Herbert Hoover’s pose “I’ll have a cake.”
While publicly praising the simple Iowan farmer's cuisine of his childhood, he dined as the chosen millionaire he became.
"He liked to eat continental cuisine, such as cucumber sauce fish," Andrew Coe said.
He ate in an almost gilded room, wearing a tuxedo.
He was unbelievably old-fashioned.
President Roosevelt would probably have made the same mistake if his wife Eleanor hadn't prompted him to set an example with his own stomach.
As the book tells it, Mrs. Roosevelt hired a housekeeper who wasn’t interested in taste at all. This led to the White House “not only serving almost the most grumpy food in all of Washington, but also in the most dismal way prepared.”
Many of the eating habits of the crisis subsided as soon as the earth got back on its feet.
The legacy remained in our constant exploration of calories and nutritional values, Ms. Ziegelman says, and in the way science is applied to cooking.
The couple say that while writing the book, they better understood Miss Ziegelman's mother's eating habits - like why she couldn't throw away food.
"She was really afraid of wasting food," Andrew Coe said.
Ziegelman commented: "The people were sick of it."
There's been an accident with wreaths.
When Eisenberg's tables were clean, she and Mr. Coe pulled out the dessert they had prepared at home: a plum dessert that President Roosevelt ate for lunch.
"It's called plum cream," Ziegelman said, adding that the usual trick was to give modest recipes elegant names.
There was no cream, just plums, a little flour, sugar, water and cinnamon.
Each reluctantly buried a spoon into thick brown matter.
Surprisingly, it was bad.
Andrew Coe added, and then again.
And as a proper householder, he said to his wife, “A little chopped walnuts would be useful.”
Europeans: Migrants are going for benefits.
