Using photoshop creates collages that humorously show what it would look like if famous series and movies were filmed in Russia.
Gilead is entitled to reimbursement of the cost of legal representation in the patent case against hepatitis C against the Merck concern
A district court judge ruled that Gilead Sciences Inc is entitled to reimbursement of attorney-at-law fees incurred by it in the trial on a C-type jaundice against drugmaker Merck & Co Inc.
In June, a verdict was annulled that Gilead was due to pay ££200million in damages to Merck's two patents in connection with Gilead, Sovaldi and Harvoni's very successful drugs, after a federal judge found repeatedly illegal conduct on the part of Merck, among other things a lie under oath and other unethical practices.
Federal Judge Beth Labson Freeman said Thursday that Gilead is entitled to waiver the fees that arose in connection with representation in the case.
Merck is trying to catch up with Gilead, which dominates the next-generation drug market against hepatitis C, which can cure more than 90 percent of patients with this liver disease.
The case dates back to 2013, when Gilead and Merck sued each other, claiming to be the owner of basic laboratory research for sofosbuvir, an active component of Gilead's drugs.
U.S.-backed troops say they have gained full control of the city of Manbij
U.S.-backed troops have gained full control of the northern city of Manbij near the border with Turkey after Islamic State's last fighters fled the city, a spokesman for the group said on Friday.
The Syrian Democratic Forces (SDF) are now combing the city after the departure of the residing group of radicals hiding in the city.
The troops liberated over 2,000 civilians that militants detained, Sharfan Darwish of the SDF Alliance and the Manbije military council, told Reuters.
“The city is now fully under our control, but we are still searching it,” he told Reuters.
The Clintons earned €10.75 million in 2015 and paid a federal tax rate of 34.2 percent
Hillarly Clinton on Friday released her 2015 tax return, stating that the Democrat presidential nominee and her husband had $10.75-million income in that year and paid a real tax of 34.2 percent.
In 2015, the Clintons donated $1 million for charity, mostly to the Clinton Foundation. Ex-President Bill Clinton contributed an amount of nearly $5.3 million in lectures to the joint box, and an earlier Secretary of State admitted an income of US$3 million from Simon & Schuster for her book about his tenure at the State Department.
The Clinton co-candidate, U.S. Senator Tim Kaine of the State of Virginia, along with his wife Anne Holton, have filed tax returns in 10 years.
They paid a real tax of 20.3 percent in 2015.
"Hillary Clinton and Tim Kaine continue to be role models of financial transparency," Jennifer Palmieri said in a statement.
Against this, Donald Trump hides behind false excuses and backs away from his earlier vows to publish his tax returns.
It's customary for U.S. presidential candidates to publish their tax returns, even if the law doesn't impose it on them.
Trump, a New York businessman, and his lawyers cited an audit of the U.S. Treasury Department's tax department as a reason for his refusal to release tax returns.
"Your move," Hillary Clinton's campaign aide said on Twitter, building on Democrat nominee's tax returns.
Michael Cohen, Trump's special adviser, told CNN Thursday that he wouldn't allow Trump to release tax returns until the audits are over.
According to Trumpek's critics, among whom is Republican presidential nominee Mitt Romney and other members of the Republican party, they agree that his rejection raises questions about his net profits, contributions to charities, business transactions and various other relationships, including relations with Russia.
Clinton speaks strongly on the issue and released a video on Friday recalling how high-ranking Republicans are urging Trump to publish his tax returns.
On Thursday, I mentioned the issue in a speech on economic issues in Michigan.
"He refuses to do what all the other presidential candidates have done for decades - to publish their tax returns," she said in front of people.
Politico said Trump has paid no or very low taxes over the past two years of the 1990s, and a New York Times journalist has quoted a number of tax advisers and accountants who say that could be the case today.
The rates of federal taxes have become a topic in the election of the president.
Clinton enforces a rule named after billionaire Warren Buffett, which would ensure that all those who earn more than $1 million in a year pay a tax of at least 30 percent.
President Barack Obama also supports the proposal.
Last week at a Clinton rally in Omaha, Nebraska, where Buffett's conglomerate Berkshire Hathaway is based, challenged Trump to meet and exchange tax returns.
Buffett said he also passed an IRS audit and that Trump is "not afraid" of the tax office, but of voters.
Clinton's campaign released tax returns retroactively until 2007.
The Clintons, who now live in Chappaqua, New York, on average paid a federal tax of an average of 32 percent between 2007 and 2014 and a combined tax of around 40.5 percent.
According to Clinton's 2015 tax return, unlike most Americans, they're just $100 in income.
Their main investment was a low-cost mutual index fund, from which the Clintons reported income on dividends and interest rates of 109,000 dollars.
