Condoms filled with chilli or fireworks to protect elephants in Tanzania
Conservations in Tanzania use an unorthodox way to prevent elephants from going to human dwellings - throwing condoms filled with chilli powder at them.
This procedure has proven successful, and the Honeyguide Foundation, which came up with the idea a few years ago, together with the U.S. environmental organization The Nature Conservancy, promotes this procedure and trains volunteers in villages in northern Tanzania to use this non-violent procedure in four steps to protect their homes and crops without harming animals.
Many of them used spears to defend themselves.
Friday is World Elephant Day, which is dedicated to the protection of these animals.
“Since they were developed by this ... toolbox, we’ve seen a change in behavior in these communities. People are much more confident that they can keep elephants out of their fields without hurting them,” Damian Bell, executive director of the Honeyguide Foundation, said in a statement.
The first and second step is to illuminate the passing elephant with a flashlight and lower the horn.
If it doesn't deter it, it'll come to throw away the so-called "chilli cloud."
Chilli powder mixed with salt is wrapped in a condom together with the firecracker, its end knotted so that only the wick looks from it.
After firing, the condom explodes and a gentle chilli powder is scattered into the air.
Usually, one whiff is enough and the elephant goes in a different direction.
The last option is to detonate the fireworks, which provokes a loud, clear explosion.
A Jewish director leaned into a hotel in Berlin for removing the code for calling Israel at the "requestance" of Arabs - RT News
A French film director of Jewish descent sparked a stir in the media after discovering that Kempinski Hotel Bristol in Berlin did not have a code for calls to Israel, according to an employee based on the "requirement" of Arab clients.
Claude Lanzmann, the author of "Shoah's "Shoah" documentary about the Holocaust, ventilated his frustration at the Kempinski Bristol hotel in an open letter to the German newspaper FAZ.
During his recent stay, Lanzmann tried to find Israel in a list of codes to call at the hotel.
However, the state was not listed among the countries to which it is possible to call directly from the room.
How is it possible that in 2016 in Berlin, Germany's capital, Israel was removed and erased?
Written by Lanzmann.
The Kempinski Bristol instead offers the ability to make calls to Israel via its own call centre.
The director was looking for an explanation and approached the hotel's reception with the problem.
Lanzman's "shocked" the answer he got, according to him.
The hotel worker replied that the measure was "decided at the discretion of Kempinski Hotels' management."
The reason for this move made the director even more annoyed.
"Most of our guests are Arabs and they have demanded that Israel's calling code be cleared," Lanzmann was quoted as saying.
According to Suddeutsche Zeitung, Israel's ambassador to Germany, Yakov Hadas-Handelsman, described the case as a "great embarrassment."
We were horrified and shocked by the events.
On its own, it's a huge embarrassment.
The fact that something like this happened in Germany, and in such a hotel chain, is an even greater disgrace.
There is no need for further explanation.
We expect the hotel to draw the right conclusions," he said.
The event provoked a strong reaction online, people called it scandalous.
Some believe the Kempinski hotel was simply "lost" in the demands of Arab customers.
“Shame on you,” reads another post, pointing out that the hotel, which is located in the noble Kurfurstendamm street, is located near the synagogue.
According to Spiegel, the hotel itself was quickly dismissed by calling the event an "oversight" and "apologizing to Lanzman."
“Never issued a direct order” does not include Israel in the list of codes for calls, the Suddeutsche Zeitung quotes the Kempinski hotel’s representative.
It adds that the call code has been added to the list
Police have used tear gas and rubber projectiles against protesting Brazilian students
A clash of students and police occurred in San Paulo, Brazil, on Thursday, when students met for a demonstration against government reforms in the public education system.
Police used tear gas and rubber bullets against protesters.
The problem escalated when government riot services tried to disperse the students, which ended with police using rubber bullets and tear gas.
Several hundred students took part in the demonstration in order to express their concerns about the plans.
Among San Paulo Governor Gerald Alckmin's proposals in order to save money is also the closure of nearly a hundred public schools and the relocation of 300,000 students.
Some of the participants in the demonstration could be seen spray-painting signs on the wall against interim President Michel Temer.
The latest protest comes just days after protesters took to the streets en masse in support of deposed President Dilma Rousseff, when a Senate in the country decided to indict her.
Trade between Russia and Iran rises 71% - RT Business
According to the Russian ambassador to Iran, Levan Dzhagaryan, trade turnover rose 70.9 percent year-on-year after the lifting of sanctions between Moscow and Tehran.
As the ambassador put it, since international sanctions against Iran were lifted, there has been an increase in interest on the part of Russian firms doing business in the country.
Russian companies are restoring existing ties with Iran, while new firms are ready to enter the market.
The further expansion of trade is, of course, one of the main priorities of bilateral cooperation with Iran," Dzhagaryan said.
He noted that during the first five months of 2016, trade turnover between Russia and Iran grew by 70.9 percent compared with the same period for 2015 and amounted to 856 million dollars.
Specifically, Russia's exports to Iran jumped 91.5 percent, amounting to US$697 million.
Russian imports from Iran increased by 16 percent to $158 million.
The jumping increase in trade was ensured by supplies of machinery, ground vehicles and weapons," the ambassador said.
Dzhagaryan also expects Russia to visit a record number of tourists from Iran this year.
Over the past year, Russia's diplomatic mission in Iran has issued around 35,000 visas to Iran's citizens.
Moreover, for the first time, Iran has been among the top 20 countries, according to the number of tourists arriving in Russia.
It can be said that the record numbers of tourists from Iran in Russia from last year will be surpassed," he said.
At the same time, Iran is visiting a relatively small number of Russians. The ambassador hopes there will be a change.
"Personally, I can recommend visiting the cities of Isfahan, Shiraz and Yazd, where they are in good condition preserved monuments of ancient Persian civilization," he said.
Callum Skinner hurries home after Britons win gold in cycling
Callum Skinner of Scotland entered the Hall of Fame last night in the men's team sprint at the Rio velodrome when the VB team's trio defended the title from London 2012.
The twenty-three-year-old Glasgow native was in the role of the brake he took behind his Scot Sir Chris Hoy, and the youngster delighted the legend as he rushed to the finish line and stole the victory at the hands of favored New Zealand.
Britain surprisingly won four years ago and Hoy won the fifth of his six Olympic gold medals.
And they repeated the same trick after finishing a modest sixth at the World Track Cycling Championships in March, even though Hoy had ended his career in the meantime.
The problem between Beijing and London was a replacement for dedicated opening racer Jamie Staff.
Hindes appeared just in time.
After Hoye's departure, a place was also vacant that Kian Emadi and Matt Crampton tried to fill.
Skinner was a man of British coaches and the search for potential has now been confirmed.
Kenny Hindes - who, along with Hoy, was champion four years ago - and with Skinner achieved an Olympic record in qualifying, but jersey was taken over by New Zealand in the first round.
And the British trio won gold in the team sprint.
Hindes was slightly behind after his round, but Kenny's team pushed forward and Skinner kept the lead.
The change of strategy was a big part of the transformation where Hindes drove to a higher conversion to slow down and allow Kenny and, in part, Skinner to keep up the pace.
Hoy watched the race from his position as a BBC commentator and when Skinner, Kenny and Hindes ascended the podium, didn't take his eyes off them.
Twenty-three-year-old Skinner, a Glasgow native, said: "It's a flattering comparison."
I'm here to get the best out of myself and honor my name.
We set an Olympic record in the final, and we beat the best ones.
It was just unbelievable.
There's no better way to win.
After triumphing alongside Kenny and Hoye in London in 2012, Hindes acknowledged that, on purpose, after a weak start, he fell on track.
He later withdrew the note and Britain's opponents had no option to appeal.
There was no such a problem this time.
Hindes said: "With all those British flags in the stands, I felt like in London."
In the last three months we've all come together as a team.
We've lost in a lot of world races, so winning the Olympics again is just amazing.
It's one event, one gold medal for a nation that has won seven out of ten competitions in Beijing and London.
If this is an indicator of progress over the past five months, then a successful five days await us at the velodrome.
With the form Kenny demonstrated, it's not inconceivable that he would repeat Hoy's success and win three gold medals at one Olympics at the same time.
It'd also be for Kenny €12 years younger than Hoy's birthday (this date seems sacred to British sport, Steve Redgrave, Mo Farah and Roger Bannister also celebrates his birthday on this day) - a total of six gold and one silver medal.
The same how much Hoy got.
As the next, Kenny will take part in the sprint, which begins today and will last another three days.
The twenty-eight-year-old Bolton native said: "Now I'm enjoying this one.
The team race is always the best.
You're winning along with others.
To tell you the truth, winning for yourself is a little lonely.
Hindes said: "If he wins the next two gold medals, he should be given a knight title."
That was the only medal race of the day, but Britain was expecting even more good news.
Sir Bradley Wiggins, Ed Clancy, Steven Burke and Owain Doull qualified as the fastest four-kilometer team race.
They finished with a time of 3:51.943.
The world record that fell in London in 2012 is 3:51.659.
The first lap and the final (22:42) take place on today's second day of the race, on which Wiggins will surely focus.
He debated with the commissioners yesterday before the qualifying race when referees called on him to measure the height.
In a similar women's race that continues and ends tomorrow, Laura Trott, Joanna Rowsell Shand, Elinor Barker and Katie Archibald of Milngavia achieved a world record with a time of 4:13.260.
Team VB relied on its strong team of track cycling and dominated the middle season of the Olympic Games and won a lot of medals.
After truly exceptional performances demonstrating complete superiority in Beijing and London, she dropped expectations during preparations for Rio.
There were dramatic events unfolding in the background, last but not least the resignation of technical director Shane Sutton in April over allegations of sexism and discrimination, and an end to the careers of greats such as Hoy or Victoria Pendleton.
However, as evidenced by the first night of races at the velodrome, the legendary reputation that the UK team is able to put in a top performance with perfect timing could be reaffirmed.
Uncertain future of the melting plant in Portland after termination of power supply contract
The future of Portland's aluminum smelting plant in Victoria - and the jobs of hundreds of workers - is uncertain because of the power plant, which supplies the business with electricity and which has reported on the intention of one of the owners of the melting plant to terminate the electricity supply contract. All electricity supply contracts are expected to be curtailed.
The move comes after Victoria's government decided earlier this year not to prolong the provision of subsidies that keep the business going.
A fixed twenty-year subsidy expires in November.
Up to 2000 jobs could be at risk at Alcoa Portland.
Up to 2000 jobs - direct or indirect - can be jeopardized when closed.
And because the melting plant consumes about 10 percent of the total electricity generated in Victoria, other avalanche impacts can occur.
Alcoa closed the Point Henren smelting plant in 2014, which last year led to the closure of the Anglesea plant.
Alcoa's smell in Portland is lost and is in danger of being closed.
The move follows several years of speculation about the company's future, originally in response to Australia's strong dollar and, more recently, because of race-producing prices.
AGL, which delivers electricity from the Loy Yang A plant in the Latrobe Valley has said it expects any of the other three owners will also decide to terminate the contract.
Majority owner of the plant is a joint venture by Alcoa and Alumina, small business shares held by Chinese company CITIC and Japan's Marubeni Corp.
"AGL expects the Portland plant to continue to operate, in other words, that the technical demand for electricity from the smelting plant will continue," she said.
AGL said the treaties were so-called "hedging contracts" that were due to come into force from November 2016.
Contracts have a 12-month notice period, so their validity actually expires in 2017.
“AGL continues to assume that the contracts will account for their profits for fiscal year 2017,” the plant said.
Any potential impact on AGL's profits from fiscal 2018 onwards is currently limited due to a strong commodity market outlook for wholesale electricity prices.
AGL said it had already drafted the value of the contracts according to it's $187-million-dollar books "in the event of termination."
Alcoa said the contract's decision to terminate "does not have an impact on its ... the ability to run a melting plant or secure the supply of electricity to a melting plant."
The U.S. company said it had entered into a 20-year contract with Loy Yang in 2010, which was due to take effect from November this year, although it has yet to explain why it backed away from the contract.
Analysts are very cautious when it comes to the possible closure of both the Portland melting plant in Victoria as well as the Tomago plant near Newcastle due to a weak economy in the sector.
AGL's head of operations, Stehpen Mikkelsen, turned to analysts earlier this week, said Portland consumed about 600 megawatts of electricity and Tomago about 900 megawatts a year.
If both of these undertakings withdrew from the market, this would mean a substantial reduction in market demand.
It wouldn't be good for us as a manufacturer," he said.
Kendall is not only the star of Kardashian's real estate TV show - it's now an envelope of the September Vogue
Kendall Jenner (20), model and the fourth daughter of Kris Kardashian-Jenner clan's mother-manager, appeared on the front page of the September issue of U.S. Vogue magazine.
Kendall, embassiser of Estee Lauder, who performed at fashion week in Milan, Paris and New York, announced the news on her Instagram account on Thursday.
"In a room full of people I love, I want to cry when I look at this front page," Kendall wrote.
Revealing the front page was followed bizarre (or simply a sequel to the humoresque by Kardashians?) staged a video featuring the entire Kardashian-Jenner family (baric rioters Rob and Caitly, and along with them all their favorite uncle from show business, producer Keeping Up Ryan Seacrest). They forgot the cover of their sisters, only so that the scene could end with a classic
It’s a played storyline of almost every “birthday” episode of a children’s TV show (Seacrest says: “Did you think we’ve forgotten about you?” with the sincerity of a cartoon rabbit on the show at eight o’clock in the morning), but Kris somehow needed to make sure that the other girls could make a living on this publicity.
Because when we look at the September issue of Vogue magazine, we have certainly entered a new Kardashian era.
Kim's older sister may have pushed Kendall into the cover of Vogue (she was on it along with her husband Kanye West in April 2014), but the headline of the magazine's September issue is a different league.
The September issue of Vogue magazine is the best-selling issue of the year.
The edition, which resembles a book for advertising purposes rather than a fashion magazine, attracts a number of advertisers (Fashionist calculated that of the 832 pages in last year’s edition, 615 pages were with advertising content).
Revealing the star, which appears on the front page, is eagerly expected, so it’s no wonder that over the past two years the magazine’s images have been leaked before its official publication.
Among the women who have appeared on the cover in the past are Naomi Campbell, Linda Evandelista or Kate Moss.
In recent years, there have certainly been more “celebrities made by models” than “models that are celebrities.”
Jennifer Lawren, an Oscar winner and Dior ambassador, appeared on the headline in 2013, a year later had the honor of Laga Gaga.
But even if it's very unlikely that Kendall - whom the magazine describes as her generation's "broke-through model - will fall into the first category, she will be in a very good company.
These celebrities aren't relatives of well-known people, not the stars of real estate shows, or the kids of today who know how to make a well edited video (albeit Jenner represents all of this).
They're demonstrably talented women who stand out in the entertainment industry.
Beyoncé, who appeared on the title page last year, had one of the strongest years of her career thanks to the release of Lemonade.
Of course, not everyone is thrilled with Jenner's success.
Her position in the world of fashion will always be difficult because of her participation in a reality show.
Stephanie Seymore, the original Victoria's Secret model in June, criticized Jenner and her colleague Gigi Hadid, calling them "whores of the moment."
These feelings flared up again with the news that Jenner would appear on the cover of Vogue.
But this dissatisfaction is out of place.
Kendall Jenner is on the cover of the September Vogue.
She's a supermodel in the true sense.
He's presenting for Mark Jacobs.
He's selling you a make-up from Estee Lauder.
She and all her sisters sell you images of themselves on their own paywalls.
She sells photos of daughter Cindy Crawford, whom she shot for LOVE magazine.
And in her free moments, she's performing on a reality show, maybe just one of the ways to see Kourtney and her children.
Community legal aid centers reject 160 000 people each year
More than 160 000 most vulnerable citizens are rejected each year in community centres of legal aid, and in the sector itself they are preparing for impacts that will have a further reduction of funds on already extremely busy services.
In Queensland, three out of five people - mostly people of advanced age, immigrants, endangered workers, victims of violence and people without funds to be able to fight unfair evictions and the like - are rejected as early as seeking free legal aid and representation.
Community legal services, which are already tense to bursting, are preparing for further cuts.
As of June next year, these legal aid centers will lose an additional 30 percent of federal funding.
James Farrell, director of Queenslad's community legal aid centers, said that according to the latest summary report of the National Association of Community Legal Aid Centers, five thousand people have been found to be rejected each year.
Mr Farrell said that without annulling the federal government's decision, the situation would worsen.
"Community legal aid centers in Queensland are chronically underfunded, which means that thousands of people are denied the vital services they need," he said.
People who have escaped from relationships with a rapist, people with excessive and crippling debts, families going through separation, workers who are unjustly dismissed - all of these people have problems with legal dimensions and this report shows that they have no chance to get the help they need.
From June 2017, the federal government will cut funding by 30 percent, which will lead to thousands more people at risk from Queensland having to focus on complex legal problems without assistance.
We call on Queenslad's prosecutor and Senator George Brandis to immediately prevent this shortening of finances from getting the help they need when they're in legal trouble.
In the past year, the government of the state has lived up to its commitment to finance legal aid up to the national average.
Now it's time to look at the needs of community centres of legal assistance, and the communities of their clients and the Palaszczuk government must invest in this important work.
A lawsuit on Donald Trump's campaign after a staff member allegedly pointed a gun
New York: A lawsuit has been filed on Donald Trump's campaign for alleged inaction after they were told that the director of operations in North Carolina in February pointed a gun at a member of staff while driving a jeep.
The incident was described in a court file handed over by Vincent Bordini, a former software campaign supervisor in the state, on Wednesday at a state court in Charlotte.
Bordini said he had been waiting for months to resolve the situation internally before concluding that "this will not happen."
Principal Earl Phillip "hinted his fingers with a pistol, put his right indicator on the trigger, and pointed the barrel at Vincent's cap," Bordini said in the lawsuit.
Bordini, following a complaint with a local and regional campaign, found that at least four other people had experienced the same with Phillip.
“Weapons don’t have to be fired to cause damage,” Bordini said in the lawsuit.
The lawsuit comes just two days after the Republican presidential nominee sparked upheaval when he told voters in North Carolina that owners and gun holders (which is regulated in the U.S. in the Second Amendment) if his opponent Hillary Clinton is elected and will be able to appoint his judges.
Trump's campaign says the media took his words out of context and that Trump did not defend the violence.
Bordini in the lawsuit seeks compensation from the campaign for negligent oversight and Phillip for endangering personal security, the illegal use of violence and causing emotional harm.
Trump's spokeswoman Hope Hicks did not immediately respond to the report asking for comment.
Attempts to get a statement from Phillip were unsuccessful.
WCCB, a local news branch in Charlotta, announced that Phillip had recently retired from the campaign.
No one has responded in a short period of time to his consulting firm Innovative Consulting Services.
Republican presidential nominee Donald Trump is running a controversial pre-election campaign.
Bordini versus Donald J. Trump For President Inc., 16-CS-14300, General Court of Justice, Superior Court Division of Mecklenburg (Charlotta).
Britain must continue to spread Olympic spirit and expand Heathrow
Heathrow will also be a source of renewal and prosperity not only for our local communities and across London, but throughout the country.
For me, Heathrow is hugely attractive, just as it was London 2012, when we made extraordinary efforts to ensure that all of our nations and regions can benefit from the Olympic Games.
The Olympic Games made huge benefits for both Startford and the East End.
And we're going to do it again by creating opportunities for local people and businesses near Heathrow.
Only for our local communities will we create 10 000 new apprenticeships, 40 000 new jobs and put an end to youth unemployment around the airport.
Just like before the 2012 London Olympics, there will be those who will think the Heathrow expansion is too big.
I've been told that Britain will never be prepared for the Olympic Games and that we will ridicule ourselves on the world podium.
Doubters were wrong in 2012, and even in the case of Heathrow, it turns out they weren't right.
It will always be necessary to overcome obstacles, but Heathrow can answer every question that concerns him.
Like the Olympic Games, Heathrow is an infrastructure project involving many people and companies, not just a few.
Expansion is an opportunity to create 180,000 new jobs, economic growth of 211 billion pounds, to leave heritage in the form of skills and employment both around the airport and across the UK.
We've got a great team on Heathrow that, in the deadline and with a set budget, introduced an 11-billion-pound infrastructure investment program, starting with the T5 terminal - which has been voted the best terminal in the world for five years in a row, after Terminal T2, built between the world's two busiest runways.
They are now ready to deliver a third runway, which costs 16 billion pounds of private investment.
The 2012 Games in London were a huge success for Great Britain and I am confident that Heathrow's expansion will be that too.
It's time for us to continue to spread the spirits of the Olympic Games and expand Heathrow, secure trading for the future and leave a legacy of the skills and employment that Britain can be proud of.
Lord Paul Deighton is the Chairman of the Board of Directors and former Treasury's Commercial Affairs Deputy.
Adblock Plus - Facebook in the cat and mouse in ad blocking
Attempts to bypass ad-blocking programs have become a cat and mouse game for companies that make money from online marketing. At least that’s what one of the companies that programs such software says.
On Thursday, just two days after Facebook announced that it was going to block ad filters on its desktop site, Adblock Plus posted a post on its pages saying that users could update the filters in their program and re-block the ad on that social network.
Facebook said on Tuesday that its desktop websites will change encoding around ads to make it more difficult for ad filters to find out which parts of the page are advertising.
Adblock, however, now claims to have bypassed the new codes.
Adblock spokesperson Ben Williams wrote in a blog post that "this kind of war between an open source ad-blocking community and those trying to bypass them has been going on since ad blocking was invented," and that Facebook is likely to come up with another custom fix.
The fact that a company owning the world's largest social network has been involved in the war only proves how much of an ad-blocking has become for the industry.
According to a study that Pagefaire conducted last year, and Adobe uses ad filters around 200 million people around the world - nearly four times that in 2013.
Mobile advertising now amounts to 84 percent of profits from advertising on Facebook, with 1.03 billion active users visiting Facebook from mobile devices every day.
The spokesperson said, however, that the company is going against desktop ad filters, because mobile ad filters for mobile apps for the social network and Instagram don't work.
Advertising on desktops still represents a profit high enough that Facebook decides to invest in it.
Facebook also said on Tuesday that it will update an ad preferences tool to give people the ability to better control how their ads appear.
On Thursday, Facebook said Adblock's new filters worried them, as they could also block content that isn't unrelated to the ad on the sites.
“It’s not a pleasant experience for people, and we want to solve this problem,” a Facebook spokesperson said in an e-mail statement.
Ad blocking is an insensitive tool, and that's why we'd rather focus on building tools like Ad Preferences that give people control.
The woman's car was hit by a Swiss highway
The victim was lying on the highway in the middle of the night.
Police are looking for witnesses to an event in which a woman who had probably tried to commit suicide was swepted on the highway of several cars on Thursday in the early hours.
The accident occurred on an A1 motorway near Morges in the canton of Vaud shortly after midnight on Thursday.
Vaud police said in a statement that the actor of the "desperate act" was lying in the dark on the road and was run over by several cars.
It is clear that the victim, a woman aged 49, wanted to kill herself and was hit by several cars, including a truck.
She died at the scene.
Several drivers have already been interviewed, but according to police, still not all have been identified. Police are urging these drivers and anyone else who witnessed the accident to turn themselves in.
A criminal investigation has been opened, a "logical" move in a situation like this, attorney Gilles Hofstetter told Le Tribune de Geneve.
It is still necessary to determine whether any of the drivers involved will face any charges.
Was it possible to prevent the impact?
That's what the prosecutor has to find out," Hofstetter said.
Based on two similar cases over the past five years, it appears that drivers won't take responsibility.
In both cases - of which in one involved a person walking along the highway - it was decided that motorists could not expect a pedestrian crossing the highway in the middle of the night, as the newspaper said.
Last year, a police officer was cleared of charges of negligent homicide after she knocked and killed a man lying on an A1 highway near Paynerne on the night of August 2014.
It was heard in court that the 22-year-old victim was drinking alcohol and fainted on the road.
The court found that a police officer - who was responding to an emergency call - drove in an appropriate manner and that the man's presence on the road was "completely exceptional and unpredictable situation," the judge said.
Anyone with information about Thursday's incident at Morges is expected to be reported to the police by calling 021 644 83 46 or 021 644 44 44.
Tolerant city.
Prague is a popular destination for the LGBT community.
The Association of Czech Travel Agencies (AČCKA) expects up to 20 thousand visitors to come to Prague as part of the Prague Pride.
Kateřina Petříčková, Executive Director of AČCKA, said this on Friday.
The sixth edition of Prague Pride began on August 8, the festival describes the life of lesbians, gays, bisexuals and transsexuals (LGBT).
About 35 thousand people attended the event last year.
"Prague is one of the most popular destinations of the LGBT community, due to a high level of tolerance and a wide range of specialized enterprises," Petříčková said.
According to a survey of organizers, foreign visitors to the festival spend an average of 3100 crowns per person every day.
Gay tourists are provided with special maps of Prague with a complete list of Prague gay and gay-friendly bars, clubs, cafes and restaurants in the form of mobile applications, etc.
"For tourism in general, LGBT clients are very lucrative, interested in stays in higher price sessions with a high standard of travel services and spend more than others," Petříčková said.
As part of the festival programme, the first Pride Tourism Symposium will take place this year.
Experts from the LGBT tourism sector from Europe, the USA and China are lectured.
The festival continues until Sunday, August 14.
The main theme this year is love, but the event will also focus on several thematic areas, which are family, AIDS prevention and transgender issues.
The highlight of the festival on Saturday will be a traditional procession with allegorical cars, which will go from Wenceslas Square to Letná.
The procession, attended by thousands of people in recent years, this year's minute's silence will honor the memory of the victims of the June nightclub nightclub in Orlando, where the attacker shooted 49 people, mostly homosexuals.
Pat and Mat celebrate forty years
The irrevocable handymen Pat and Mat, whose efforts usually end in disaster, have been entertaining both small and large viewers for forty years.
The pilot of this bedtime series by director Lubomír Beneš premiered on August 12, 1976.
After the creation of this animated series, which is now known to audiences in more than a hundred countries of the world, there were cartoon jokes by the artist and director Lubomír Beneš, which he invented for a magazine with two handymans Mr. Ouholíček and Sedlec.
Beneš then invited the dramaturg Jiří Kubíček and the artist Vladimír Jiránek to cooperate and together they created the film Kuťáci.
Today's form with a yellow and red trick and a beanie and radiography was given to the characters in 1979, when a series for Slovak Television began to be created in Short Film Prague.
The names Pat and Mat were then given to DIY in 1985, this time it was filmed directly for the short film Prague.
In 1990 Lubomír Beneš founded his own studio of the animated film AIF Studio in Prague and Zurich, where other works were created.
After his death in 1995, other works continued with other creators, many of them involved Beneš's son Marek.
There are currently more than ninety episodes with stories of these handymens, which everything sprinkles under their hands, but they are never angry with each other and are always full of optimism, ideas and DIY enthusiasm, and in the end they are satisfied with their work, which is evidenced by the familiar gesture "...and that's it!".
Pat and Mat have even moved from TV screens to books, DVDs to theater, and characters or other toys depicting Pata and Mata are a hit of every toy store.
Fairy tale for Fiji and two-year-old Harmony.
The end of the week is marked by fairy tales, we have chosen for you one sports and one of our lives.
They're both having a good ending.
Well, let's take a look.
Fiji has achieved historic success.
What is the reason for the joy of the whole country
