Rugby selection of this archipelago defeated the Great Britain team in the final of the Olympic tournament of seven rugby.
It's the first medal that Fiji won for themselves at the Olympics.
Waiting for winning the first precious metal took sixty years after sending the first expedition to the Olympic Games.
A great credit for the success is borne by Coach Fiji.
Ironically, however, it is Brit Ben Ryan.
He has been leading the team since 2013 and after the final as a British citizen he could have been satisfied, unlike his compatriots.
"Let's play freely and lightly and see if we can handle the biggest game in our history," the coach declared before the final.
Fiji eventually dominated it, and a small state with 900 thousand inhabitants is now experiencing an Olympic fairy tale.
The report was carried by the French newspaper Le Monde.
New friend and happiness for a little girl.
Two-year-old Harmonie-Rose Ivy Allen, who has been living without her arms and legs since 11 months, has received a new friend.
A doll that looks like her.
"Mom, she's like me," the two-year-old girl rejoiced, as her mother told ABC News.
She added that her daughter liked to take off the doll's arms and legs and then sit together without prosthetics.
Harmony in less than a year's life was diagnosed with type B meningitis, doctors gave her only a ten percent chance of survival.
There's nothing I don't love about her.
She's beautiful, she's smart, she's funny and she's never giving up.
It also has an outlook.
I've senced her introducing herself to the other children with the words Hi, I'm Harmony and I don't have hands, my mother added.
See how much joy the doll has generated.
