Rowing Sovereigns.
We're just going to win, Murray and Bond were laughing.
Rowers Eric Murray and Hamish Bond from New Zealand have reigned in the category of doubles without a helmsman for seven years and have confirmed their dominance at the Olympic Games in Rio de Janeiro.
At first glance, the disparate duo switched to a smaller crew after the Beijing Olympics and have been crushing competition since 2009.
On Rodrigo de Freitas' lagoon, they claimed their 69th win in a row in Thursday's final.
In what way are we different from others?
We're just prettier, Murray joked already during the Olympic regatta, when he was supposed to answer questions about the dominance of the New Zealand duo.
Frankly, we're not doing anything different from others.
We're just training hard, very hard.
And we're constantly trying to improve our technique, that's our philosophy, Murray said more seriously.
Their dominance is also remarkable because they are physically different.
Murray is a nearly two-metre and hundred-kilometer chasnik, an academic-looking Bond is five centimetres smaller and 15 kilograms lighter.
Moreover, they have a different nature.
Bond is studying and doing his finances, Murray, for example, has had a charity boxing fight.
They don't spend much time together outside of rowing.
Eric's basically getting into the ship saying we can't lose.
We're just gonna win.
I tend to think about what can go wrong, admitted Bond, who even at a press conference clearly demonstrated that Murray was dominant in the crew.
He was always the first to answer, and Bond was waiting for his opportunity.
On the water, however, it destroys opponents with calm force.
They're running their pace, and although they're often still not in the lead in the middle of the track, the opponents haven't found a recipe for their gradual acceleration.
We're racing on a two-kilometre track and we never wanted to drive the first kilometre at all.
We're just trying to drive a pace that no one else can.
We know what we have to do in order to win, the thirty-four-year-old Murray said.
With his four-year-old partner, he won seven gold medals at the World Championships.
They also triumphed in 2007, when they were both members of the Golden Four without a helmsman.
Just after the unexpected failure of a larger crew at the Beijing Olympics a year later, they decided to switch to two without a helmsman.
At the London Olympics, they probably atoned for the biggest defeat of their careers and confirmed their dominance in Rio.
London was very intense.
We did everything the coach told us and didn't ask questions.
It's been a little more fun this time.
We knew it was going to be bloody hard, and we had to make sure we were mentally well, Murray stated.
We know they all want to overthrew us from the throne.
We've got the target on our backs all the time.
But we're trying to be half a step ahead of everyone, he added.
The climate model according to NASA: Venus was once habitable
According to projections of NASA's new climate model, Venus was once habitable.
Scientists at NASA's Goddard Institute for Space Studies have developed a model to examine Venus using tools and algorithms similar to those used by climate-study scientists.
The model was designed to determine whether conditions similar to those on Earth may have prevailed once on Venus, whose surface is hot, without water and saturated with CO2.
Scientists have previously suggested that Venus's low rotational speed can be explained by its dense atmosphere.
According to the latest simulations, however, it seems that Venus once boasted a sparse Earth-like atmosphere while spinning slowly.
The model also dealt with the earlier topography of Venus.
On the planet, areas were probably drier than on Earth, but there was once plenty of water and geographical diversity on it to control life and provide protection against rapid evaporation.
Venus previously also used the Sun, which was 30 percent more subdued.
“In a simulation of the GISS Venus model during a slow rotation, it exposes its daily side to the sun for almost two months at a time,” GISS scientist Anthony Del Genio explained in a new report.
In this way, the surface is heated and rain forms, creating a dense layer of clouds that behave like an umbrella and protect the surface from excessive solar heat.
The result is a climate with average temperatures, which are actually slightly lower than currently on Earth.
The model compiled the story of Venus, similar to evolution, previously hypothetically compiled by planetary scientists.
Even in the past, Venus was probably habitable -- over the course of billions of years -- it was exposed to excessive heat and ultraviolet radiation.
Its oceans evaporated and water vapors were burned with ultraviolet light.
Hydrogen has escaped, leaving only a dense atmosphere of carbon dioxide with the deadly effect of greenhouse gas -- that's Venus as we know it today.
The researchers published their results from the new climate model of Venus in the journal Geophysical Research Letters.
Patrick Roy has resigned as coach of the Colorado Avalanche team
Patrick Roy resigned on Thursday as coach and vice-president of the Colorado Avalanche team's hockey operations, citing as a reason the impossibility to participate in decision-making on team issues.
Colorda's executive vice/general manager Joe Sakic confirmed the move, saying the club would start looking for a new coach immediately.
"For the last three years, I've served as head coach and vice president of hockey operations for the Colorado Avalanche, with energy, passion and determination," fifty-year-old Roy said in a statement.
For a long time and intensely during the summer, I wondered how I could improve the team, give it the depth it needs, and take it to the next level.
In order to achieve this, it is necessary that the visions of the coach and vice president of hockey operations are consistent with the organization’s vision.
They must also have the opportunity to participate in decisions that have an impact on the team's performance.
These conditions aren't currently met.
Sakic, who received Roy on May 23, 2013, thanked him for his team's contributions.
"Patrick informed me of his decision today," Sakic said.
We appreciate everything he has done for our organization, and we wish him good luck in the future.
Roy has been nominated to win by Jack Adams as the league's best coach during his first season with the 2013/2014 Avalanche.
He led the team to score 112 points and first place in the Central Division. Colorado, however, faced Minnesota in the first round of the playoffs, and during the last two years he has always failed to score games after the season.
The Avalanche team scored 39:39:4 in the 2015/2016 season and was missing five points to win a second wild card in the western conference.
Goalie Roy, who enrolled in the Hall of Fame, won the Stanley Cup four times -- twice with the Montreal Canadiens and twice with the Avalanche.
Workers on an oil rig in the North Sea are resuming talks and interrupting a strike
Top leaders of the negotiating parties said strikes because of wages for North Sea oil rig workers are being interrupted, while all sides of the dispute met for a new round of negotiations.
Strikes against the Wood Group oil service company were sporadically carried out on rigs in the North Sea operated by Royal Dutch Shell.
"Wood Group and unions Unite and RMT can confirm that they have agreed to renew the process for resolving current disagreements," the statement said.
Starting next week, we'll have a series of all-day meetings over the next few weeks where we'll try to look at the problems from new angles.
According to trade unions, their recent action, which they said was the first in more than 25 years, is in protest against wage cuts by up to 30 percent.
Wood Group has cut rates this year and paid out about 9 percent less to its suppliers in the North Sea because of negative market pressure.
The company's rate cut comes after a 10 percent cut for coastal companies in 2014.
The company argues that further cuts would give the company room to cope with a market decline.
Shell, on the other hand, removed thousands of people from its payroll and announced plans to abandon oil and gas operations in up to 10 countries due to modernizing its processes - immediately after a mega merger with British energy company BG Group.
Energy companies are under pressure because of low oil prices, the economic situation associated with Britain's June decision to leave the European Union.
House member from Scotland, Dave Anderson, met with union leaders earlier this week to discuss one of the greatest working disagreements of an entire generation that has led to a strike in the North Sea.
The North Sea, he said, is vitally important for the region's economy.
"In the North Sea and across the country, we need an economy that works for everyone, not just for some," he said in a statement.
By comparison, nearly $1.4 billion is expected to invest in new North Sea projects in 2016, while the average over the past five years is around US$7 billion.
Kepler measures the rotation speed of stars in Pleiades
Kepler's latest mission to help astronomers is to obtain a near-complete list of star rotation speeds from the Pleiades cluster.
New data helps astronomers understand why the stars in the cluster spin at different speeds, as well as how the rotation time relates to the formation of the planet.
"We hope that by comparing our results with other clusters, we will learn more about the relationship between stellar matter, its age, and even the history in its solar system," Luise Rebull, a researcher at Caltech's Infrared Processing and Analysis Center in Pasadena, said in a new report.
Cluster Pleiades is located 445 light-years from Earth.
Many stars in clusters are now reaching a state of maturity and have formed about 125 million years.
Now it's turning the fastest.
At moments of maximum rotating speeds, these teenage stars release huge amounts of stellar wind.
Because these winds move along the magnetic fields of stars, they have a slowing effect on them.
As the star grows older, so does its rotation speed.
Kepler measures each star's rotation rate by measuring the time between discovering recognizable points on stars -- similar to sunspots.
According to the latest data on rotational speeds, larger stars rotate more slowly than less massive stars.
Rebulla compares stars to dancers, bigger pirouettes more slowly than smaller ones.
“In the “ballet”, we can see that slower-spinning stars are more massive, while those that spin the fastest are usually very light stars,” Rebull said.
Because the Pleiades is so close, it serves as an ideal cosmic lab to compare the quality of the stars and the speed of rotation.
“Pleiades give us an anchor for theoretical models of stellar rotation in both directions, younger and older stars,” Rebull said.
There's still a lot we want to learn about how, when, and why the stars are slowing down their rotation speed and, so to speak, "hang their dancing boots on the cloves."
Rebull and her colleagues have detailed the latest analysis of Pleiade rotation speeds in three studios, which will soon be published in the Astronomical Journal.
Is a qualifying agreement necessary?
If I didn't apply, then the qualifying agreement wouldn't close, and after a few months I could go to another hospital?
Is it even possible to work after graduation without applying to specialization education?
My point is that in the field in which I would like to work, a vacancy is now only in a more distant hospital, but in about half a year it might be possible to vacate space in another, where I would like to work afterwards.
So I don't know how to deal with the situation for about half a year, the question continues.
Responds Mgr. Lenka Vlková.
No legislation stipulates an obligation to conclude a qualifying agreement with an employer - a hospital.
The Qualification Agreement is a legal institute regulated by the Labour Code and can be concluded between an employee and an employer in order to ensure a return on the costs incurred associated with increasing or deepening the employee’s qualifications.
In the field of health care, there is long-term pressure on the non-closure of these agreements in view of ensuring the working mobility of healthcare workers, and not all hospitals currently require this agreement.
Pursuant to Act No. 95/2004 Coll., on the conditions for the acquisition and recognition of professional competence and specialized competence to pursue the medical profession of a doctor, a dentist and a pharmacist, participation in specialization education is considered as a deepening of qualifications (not an increase).
This means that the qualification agreement can be concluded according to the Labour Code only if the costs amount to at least 75 000 Kč.
No legal regulation even stipulates the obligation to apply for specialization education.
The law only stipulates the conditions of the application for inclusion in the field of specialization education.
However, according to the law, the doctor is entitled to pursue the profession of a doctor already thanks to the completion of the necessary university studies, although under statutory conditions (under the professional supervision of a doctor with specialized competence in the relevant field).
The law foresees a situation where professional experience completed in another field of specialization may be included in the specialization education of the doctor, if its content and scope correspond to the relevant educational program.
In theory, it would be possible to calculate the time worked elsewhere, which would correspond to the interviewer's situation.
However, in view of the fact that I do not know the closer circumstances of this case, it can be recommended to consult with the relevant Department of the Ministry of Health (Department of Science and Medical Occupations), since the Ministry of Health by law provides an advisory and consulting service related to inclusion in specialization education and its course.
The advisory service is also provided free of charge by the Czech Medical Chamber, of which every doctor must be a member.
Doctors from Halab, Syria, are appealing for U.S. intervention.
The United States has made nome efforts to end the siege (rebel parts of the city by government troops), and has not used its influence to enforce the protection of civilians.
"We don't need tears, compassion, and even prayers; however, we desperately need to create a no-fly zone over eastern Halab to stop the attacks, and we need international action to ensure that Halab is not encircled again," the doctors said.
Russia's forces, who support an army loyal to President Bashar al-Assad, have announced as of today, stopping strikes in the Halabu region every day for three hours to allow safe passage to humanitarian convoys.
From 9:00 to 12:00 CEST, all military activities, air strikes and artillery fire are to be stopped.
Stephen O'Brien, head of U.N. humanitarian operations, welcomed this, but stressed that at least a two-day ceasefire was needed.
According to AFP, 15 out of 35 doctors operating in the eastern part of Halab, already controlled by rebels, appealed to Obama in the letter.
They write that during the five-year war they witnessed the violent, cruel death of countless patients, friends and colleagues.
The world observes and notes how complex Syria is, but it does little to protect us.
Recent offers of evacuating residents from the Syrian regime and Russia sound like only slightly obscured threats, pediatricians, surgeons and other doctors said.
Doctors said there have been 42 attacks on medical facilities in Syria over the past month, including 15 on hospitals in which they work.
We're most sad that we have to decide who's going to live and who's going to die.
Young children with such serious injuries come to the emergency room that we have to choose among them those most likely to survive.
And sometimes we don't even have the necessary material to help them, doctors described.
Two weeks ago, four newborns suffocated when an explosion interrupted supplies of oxygen to their incubators.
They gasped, and then their lives were over - before he really could begin, they reminded.
An army loyal to President Bashar al-Assad, backed by Russian forces, has encircled in recent weeks by insurgent-dominated eastern Halab, with more than 200,000 people without food supplies inside.
The rebels, who are under constant strikes by the Russian and Syrian air forces, managed on Saturday after three weeks to break the encirclement and launch a counter-offensive.
Both sides were then added reinforcements in the city and its surroundings.
